{"id": "297d5c52-e555-42bc-9571-9a283a0df8c4", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:41:03.265185", "source": "user", "parent_id": "57741d67-34f9-4470-9791-3ab6b9eb4dd5", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task. Please do NOT run experiments or fabricate numerical results; the submission contract requires a final experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility), not execution. Conduct a careful initial inspection and respond with the following three sections only, concisely but specifically. Save any supporting notes under /workspace under a clearly named scratch file (e.g., notes/initial_inspection.md) and reference it in your reply if useful, but do not start writing the final design yet. If the network is disabled, work purely from local files and the known scope of TorchOpt and implicit-differentiation meta-learning literature; do not invent combinations of APIs that are not supported by TorchOpt, and clearly flag any such guess as an assumption to be validated later. Cite specific TorchOpt modules/classes/algorithms (e.g., MetaSGD, implicit MAML via functional API, differentiable meta-learning via torchopt.MetaGradientModule / Optax implicit-grad wrappers, etc.) only if you can justify them from the local repository or installed package. Sections requested: (1) Repository and source inspection — describe what you find under /workspace (files, any existing meta-learning code, TorchOpt installation state). If TorchOpt or relevant optimizers are not installed under this bubblewrap/Python environment, note that explicitly and propose a fallback design that is feasible within the stated environment (system Python, no network). If TorchOpt source/docs are present locally, summarize the relevant API surface for implicit differentiation (e.g., functional / differentiable / implicit-meta interfaces, Hessian-vector products, conjugate gradient, Neumann series). (2) Proposed experimental design outline (high-level, not yet approved) — list the research question restated in operational terms, the candidate implicit-differentiation approach(es) in TorchOpt you would compare, the baseline(s) (e.g., explicit/forward-mode MAML or first-order MAML/FOMAML), the task suite (toy regression, sine-wave few-shot, MiniImageNet/CIFAR-FS if feasible offline, otherwise synthetic), the meta-training algorithm and inner-loop optimizer choice, the outer-loop update rule and any implicit-solver hyperparameters (CG iterations, damping, Neumann depth), the number of inner-adaptation steps, the learning rates / batch sizes, number of meta-iterations, seeds, and the metrics (meta-test accuracy/loss, per-step inner loss trajectories, wall-clock/iteration cost, memory footprint). (3) Assumptions and uncertainties — enumerate unresolved setup questions (e.g., which TorchOpt version and which implicit-differentiation interface is intended; whether implicit differentiation targets the inner-loop initialization or the meta-parameters; whether second-order information is required or first-order implicit is acceptable; whether the benchmark is regression, classification, or both; compute budget; expected empirical outcome thresholds for declaring success), and state explicitly which of these block approving the design and which are minor implementation defaults you can choose yourself with a stated rationale. Do not initiate the main design work yet; I will review this outline and approve or request focused clarification before you proceed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How can you implement implicit differentiation for meta-learning using TorchOpt?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"netw