{"id": "c41d41fb-b0a1-441d-9446-2fa7e016b19c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:42:31.583628", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e23e3c48-2f84-4972-997b-d85c1902b3ae", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-ae2d4f2b-abba-44d6-80da-edc9963779e6", "tool_call": {"id": "call-ae2d4f2b-abba-44d6-80da-edc9963779e6", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Delivering initial inspection + plan outline\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n# Initial Inspection & Plan Outline — Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\n## 1. Repository & Source Inspection\\n\\n**Workspace state (`/workspace`).** Only `/workspace/task.json` (375 B) is present. Its embedded `track.md` reads: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"* No source tree, no cloned TorchOpt, no docs bundle, no `notes/` (I created `notes/initial_inspection.md` to record probes).\\n\\n**Environment probes.**\\n- Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, ~707 GB free; bubblewrap; **network disabled** (`curl https://pypi.org` → \\\"Could not resolve host\\\").\\n- `pip` is not on PATH (only the systemd pipe units and `/lib/python3/dist-packages/pip` source dir were found).\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import X\\\"` resolves to ModuleNotFoundError for: `torch`, `torchopt`, `numpy`, `scipy`.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 6 -iname 'torchopt*'` and `-iname 'torch' -type d` returned zero hits.\\n- No offline dataset mirrors present.\\n\\n**TorchOpt install state.** TorchOpt is **not installed** in this bubblewrap and cannot be installed here (no pip binary reachable, no network). No TorchOpt source/docs are available locally for me to cite from. Therefore every TorchOpt-specific class/function name I propose below is **a known-from-public-docs API surface, not a locally verified one**, and I will flag each such reference with \\\"proposed — validate against your installed TorchOpt version.\\\"\\n\\n**Locally verified facts (no TorchOpt).** None of the proposed TorchOpt symbols are locally verified. The only things *verified here* are positive environmental negatives: TorchOpt ∉ installed packages, TorchOpt ∉ filesystem search, network ∉ available, pip CLI ∉ available.\\n\\n**Feasibility fallback (so the design is not empty if TorchOpt isn't usable).** Two-tier plan the researcher can choose between:\\n- **Tier A — TorchOpt-native (default if a TorchOpt env is provided later).** Use TorchOpt's differentiable / functional meta-learning interface: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule` / `torchopt.MetaSGD`, the functional inner-loop with stop-gradient for the inner trajectory, and an implicit outer solver. Specific entry points I'll iterate on after approval: `torchopt.diff.implicit_diff` style wrapper, `MetaGradientModule(meta_net, inner_opt, ...)`, and the conjugate-gradient / Neumann helpers exposed by TorchOpt's differentiable-meta APIs. *All flagged as proposed — to be validated.*\\n- **Tier B — TorchOpt-free, functorch-based (the design I can fully specify without source access).** Same algorithmic story expressed with `torch.func` (functional call + `grad` + `vmap`), the standard implicit-grad identity `∂θ*/∂φ = (I − α · ∂²L_inner/∂θ²)⁻¹ · α · ∂²L_inner/∂θ∂φ` solved by either truncated Neumann series (`K` terms) or conjugate gradient with Tikhonov damping `λI`. This is mechanically equivalent to what TorchOpt's implicit wrapper hides, and the experiment harness can be structured so that swapping Tier B's hand-written solver for TorchOpt's wrapper later is a one-file change behind a single `meta_solver.py` interface. **Because no TorchOpt is installed and the rule says I must not invent unsupported APIs, Tier B is what I would specify concretely; Tier A's TorchOpt-specific call sites are kept as labeled placeholders awaiting researcher confirmation.**\\n\\n## 2. Proposed Experimental Design Outline (high-level, NOT yet approved)\\n\\n**Research question (restated operationally).** *Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt produce meta-gradients that (a) match the analytic / second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance ε, (b) train a meta-learner to comparable or better held-out accuracy than explicit (unrolled) MAML and FOMAML given a fixed inner-step budget, and (c) do so with lower wall-clock per meta-iteration and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?*\\n\\n**Candidate implicit-diff approaches to compare (k = 5 arms).**\\n1. **`EXPLICIT-MAML`** (unroll-K, retain_graph; Finn et al. 2017) — upper-bound on accuracy, baseline for cost.\\n2. **`FOMAML`** (unroll-K, first-order, detach inner grads) — first-order upper-bound baseline.\\n3. **`IMPLICIT-NEUMANN-K`** — implicit-diff via truncated Neumann series with depth K ∈ {3, 5, 10}.\\n4. **`IMPLICIT-CG-T`** — implicit-diff via conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system, outer iterations T ∈ {5, 10, 20}.\\n5. **`IMPLICIT-EXACT`** — when feasible on the small models, solve the linear system exactly as a feasibility / oracle reference (smallest toy only; not run on the image tasks).\\n\\nAll implicit arms share the same inner loss L_inner, inner optimizer (SGD with inner LR α), inner-step budget (K_init steps used only to reach the regime where the fixed-point approximation is realistic; the *meta-gradient itself* comes from the implicit solve, not from the unrolling), and meta-batch size.\\n\\n**Baselines.** `EXPLICIT-MAML` (reference for gradient fidelity), `FOMAML` (reference for first-order cost), and an *analytic* gradient on the toy regression (closed-form Jacobian of the 1-step linear inner loop) as an oracle for grad-match metrics.\\n\\n**Task suite (CPU-feasible; no network).**\\n- **Toy linear regression** (`y = w*·x + ε`, K=1 inner step): provides a closed-form Jacobian, lets us measure ‖g_implicit − g_analytic‖₂ directly. Synthetic, instant.\\n- **Sine-wave few-shot regression** (Finn et al. 2017 protocol: amplitude ∈ [0.1, 5.0], phase ∈ [0, π], K_shot = 5, K_query = 10, 4-gradient-update inner steps). Synthetic.\\n- **Sinusoid classification / Omniglot N-way K-shot** is preferred if an offline mirror is supplied; otherwise a **synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot benchmark** (mixture of Gaussians in R^d, controllable inter-class margin) is used so the experiment is reproducible without downloads.\\n\\n**Network.** MLP with 2 hidden layers of 40 units and `ReLU` for the regression arms; a 4-layer ConvNet (matching the standard MiniImageNet MAML conv) for the classification arm. All small enough to fit in CPU memory with batch size up to 4 tasks of size 25.\\n\\n**Inner-loop optimizer.** `torchopt.MetaSGD` is the default candidate (per-parameter inner LR), with `torch.optim.SGD` as the fallback if the TorchOpt API is unavailable in the target env. Inner LR α ∈ {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}; outer LR β = 0.001 (Adam for the meta-optimizer).\\n\\n**Outer-loop / implicit-solver hyperparameters.**\\n- Damping λ ∈ {0.0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} applied as `(H + λI)` to keep the system well-conditioned (Lorraine et al. 2020, Rajeswaran et al. 2019, iMAML).\\n- Neumann depth K ∈ {3, 5, 10}; CG iterations T ∈ {5, 10, 20}; CG tolerance 1e-5.\\n- Hessian-vector products via double-backward `torch.autograd.grad` (or functorch's `jvp`/`vjp`), avoiding ever materializing the full Hessian.\\n\\n**Inner-adaptation steps.** K_inner ∈ {1, 5, 10}*; sweeping K_inner lets us check the claim \\\"implicit-diff decouples K_inner from memory cost.\\\" *(*K_inner is the number of inner steps at *inference/adaptation*; for the implicit arms, the *meta-gradient* is independent of K_inner at the fixed point.)\\n\\n**Learning rates / batch sizes.** Meta batch (tasks per step) ∈ {2, 8, 32} for the regression arms; ∈ {2, 4} for the conv arm. Outer LR β = 0.001.\\n\\n**Meta-iterations & seeds.** 5,000 meta-iterations on the toy, 10,000 on sine, 20,000 on classification. Seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm; mean ± std reported.\\n\\n**Metrics.**\\n- *Gradient fidelity:* ‖g_implicit(λ,K) − g_explicit‖₂ / ‖g_explicit‖₂ on the toy (where g_explicit is the unrolled-K second-order gradient, and g_analytic is the closed form for K=1).\\n- *Solver convergence:* CG residual ‖r‖₂ per outer iter; Neumann tail ‖(I − H̃)ᴷ g₀‖₂.\\n- *Meta-test performance:* few-shot accuracy / MSE on a held-out 600-task test split, with confidence intervals from the 3 seeds.\\n- *Inner-loss trajectories:* mean ± std of L_query vs. inner adaptation step on the test support/query split (checks that the implicit arm can match EXPLICIT-MAML's adapted parameters' loss curve).\\n- *Compute cost:* wall-clock seconds per meta-iteration; peak resident memory (`tracemalloc` / `resource.getrusage` on CPU); inner-tape memory = bytes held by `retain_graph` graph (measured as parameter count × K_inner × dtype-size, since the implicit arm *should* be O(params) rather than O(params × K_inner)).\\n- *Stability:* fraction of iterations where the implicit solve diverged, under each λ and K.\\n\\n**Analysis plan.**\\n1. *Gradient-fidelity table:* rows = arm, columns = K_inner; cell = mean ‖g_implicit − g_explicit‖ rel-error with std across seeds.\\n2. *Meta-test curve:* learning curves (meta-test acc/MSE vs. meta-iteration) for all 5 arms; log-wall-clock on the x-axis of a second plot to expose speed-accuracy tradeoff.\\n3. *Pareto front:* per-arm (peak meta-test, wall-clock/iter, peak memory) tuples; check whether any implicit arm dominates EXPLICIT-MAML on at least one axis.\\n4. *Ablations over λ and K (Neumann depth) / T (CG iters):* verify the λ ≠ 0 improves conditioning and that K=5 or T=10 is enough given α < 1/(Lipschitz of the inner Hessian).\\n5. *Failure-mode log:* for each divergence event record (α, λ, K, K_inner) and the linear-system residual at the last outer step — supports a discussion of when implicit-diff pays off.\\n\\n**Controls.**\\n- Random-seed control (3 seeds per arm).\\n- Same inner-step count across arms within a task tier.\\n- Shared inner-network initialization distribution (deterministic-given-seed).\\n- Data-generation RNG and task-sampling RNG separated so test tasks are held out and unseen.\\n- Sanity probe: with α = 0, every implicit arm must return the FOMAML gradient (degenerate control).\\n\\n**Feasibility check (no-env estimate).**\\n- Toy regression + sine-wave both run on CPU in seconds to minutes per arm × seed (tiny networks, K_inner ≤ 10).\\n- Synthetic N-way K-shot classification with the 4-layer ConvNet at meta-batch 4 tasks × 25 examples is the heavy arm; estimated minutes per meta-iteration on the 24-core box, so 20K iters × 5 arms × 3 seeds is the dominant cost — multi-hour, but well within an overnight CPU budget. Cannot be run in this bubblewrap (no torch) but the design size is CPU-realistic.\\n- Memory: implicit arm stores only O(params) extra activations vs. EXPLICIT-MAML's O(params · K_inner); this is asserted in the design and is itself a result to be measured.\\n\\n## 3. Assumptions & Uncertainties\\n\\n**A. Blocking questions (must be answered before I write the final design).**\\n1. **Which TorchOpt version / which entry point is intended?** I have not found TorchOpt locally; I need the researcher to confirm the intended interface among: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule`, `torchopt.diff.implicit_diff`-style wrapper, `torchopt.MetaSGD` + manual CG/Neumann, or an Optax-style `optax.implicit_diff` (if the Optax ↔ TorchOpt wrapper is the intended surface). My plan above includes a TorchOpt-free Tier B that I can fully specify now and that maps 1-to-1 onto whatever TorchOpt helper the researcher confirms, so the rest of the design is robust to this answer.\\n2. **What is the target of differentiation?** (a) Only the inner-loop initialization θ₀ (classical iMAML), (b) per-parameter inner learning rates and initialization (MetaSGD-style), or (c) outer/hyperparameters like regularization weight? My outline assumes (a) with (b) as a secondary arm.\\n3. **Compute budget & expected success thresholds.** What's the wall-clock ceiling per arm, and what meta-test gap vs. EXPLICIT-MAML is acceptable for declaring the implicit arm \\\"successful\\\" (e.g., within 1 pp on classification, within 0.005 MSE on sine, ‖g‖ rel-error ≤ 1e-2 on the toy)? These thresholds belong in the final design.\\n4. **Tasks & data.** Are offline mirrors of MiniImageNet / CIFAR-FS / Omniglot available, or must I stay synthetic? My fallback is a synthetic Gaussian-mixture few-shot benchmark; if real-task data is available the classification arm should switch to it.\\n5. **Tier decision.** Should the deliverable specify Tier A (TorchOpt calls literally named, with API names marked as proposed-to-validate) or Tier B (functorch code that is fully specifiable without source access and is wrapped so a TorchOpt call can be slotted in once confirmed)?\\n\\n**B. Non-blocking defaults I will choose myself with stated rationale (and that the researcher can override at approval time).**\\n- Inner optimizer = SGD with per-parameter LR (MetaSGD where available, plain SGD fallback).\\n- Damping λ swept over {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} with 1e-2 as default (matches iMAML's reported regime).\\n- Neumann depth K = 5; CG iters T = 10 as defaults (typical neighboring-paper defaults).\\n- Meta-optimizer = Adam, β = 0.001 (standard).\\n- 3 seeds, meta-test set of 600 held-out tasks.\\n- All small enough for CPU (no GPU dependency in the design), since GPU is disabled.\\n- Gradient-fidelity metric on the toy uses the K=1 closed form; on other tasks it uses unrolled-K second-order as the reference.\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture few-shot if no real dataset mirror is supplied.\\n\\n**C. Known gotchas I'll address in the final design (not blockers, listed for transparency).**\\n- `retain_graph=True` is required for the EXPLICIT arm; memory cost will be reported separately from grad-error so it doesn't contaminate the gradient-fidelity metric.\\n- For deeper inner loops, the Neumann series only converges if α · ‖H‖₂ < 1; I will verify this condition empirically before drawing conclusions about K.\\n- Truncated Neumann + CG are not the only options (there is also the Gauss–Newton/BFGS implicit-meta family); I include them only if there is room, as a sensitivity arm, *after* the primary five-arm comparison is locked in.\\n\\n**Status.** Awaiting researcher approval on (1)–(5) in section A before drafting the full design (rationale, controls, full hyperparameter table, analysis plan with statistical tests, and the concrete Tier-A vs. Tier-B implementation strategy). No experiment has been run; no outcomes are claimed.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"\\n- Environment probe output (Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, ~707 GB free, no pip on PATH, no torch/torchopt/numpy/scipy, network disabled via `curl https://pypi.org` → \\\"Could not resolve host\\\", `find /` found no torchopt/torch dirs).\\n- `/workspace/task.json` content confirms a design-only scope; its embedded `documents.track.md` says \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- Scope notes saved to `/workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md` (46 lines) for the researcher's reference.\\n- No locally verified TorchOpt APIs: every TorchOpt symbol named in the plan is proposed-from-public-docs and explicitly flagged for researcher confirmation against the actual installed TorchOpt version.\\n\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Default inner optimizer = SGD-with-per-parameter-LR (torchopt.MetaSGD candidate, torch.optim.SGD fallback); meta-optimizer = Adam, β = 0.001.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"First-order implicit (e.g., setting damping high or skipping the Gauss–Newton inversion) is acceptable as a within-arm condition; second-order implicit is the primary arm but not a hard requirement.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Implicit gradient target is the inner-loop initialization θ₀ (the classical iMAML setting), with MetaSGD-style per-parameter inner LR as a secondary arm.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"No offline dataset mirrors of MiniImageNet / CIFAR-FS / Omniglot are present, so the classification arm will use a synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot benchmark unless the researcher supplies data.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Compute is CPU-only with 24 cores and is feasible for the proposed small-network arms (MLP-40/ConvNet-4-layer) within a multi-hour overnight budget; GPU is not used.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"TorchOpt is NOT installed in this bubblewrap and cannot be installed (no pip, no network) — therefore every TorchOpt API name in the plan is a public-docs-based proposal, not a locally verified fact.</item>\\n\"},{\"item\":\"Tier B (functorch-based, TorchOpt-free) is a valid placeholder that maps 1-to-1 onto Tier A; swapping in the real TorchOpt call later is a single-file change behind a `meta_solver.py` interface.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Minor: second-order vs. first-order implicit acceptability, Neumann depth default (5 vs. 10), CG iters default (10 vs. 20), damping λ default sweep grid, number of seeds (3 vs. 5).</item>\",\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: target of differentiation (initialization θ₀ vs. per-parameter inner LR vs. other meta-parameters).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: which Tier (A: TorchOpt-literal, B: functorch-with-TorchOpt-slot) the researcher wants the deliverable to specify concretely.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: whether real few-shot datasets can be supplied offline, or whether the synthetic Gaussian-mixture benchmark is acceptable.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: compute budget ceiling per arm and the empirical success thresholds (gap to EXPLICIT-MAML on acc/MSE, allowed ‖g_implicit − g_explicit‖ rel-error).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: which TorchOpt version / entry point is intended (MetaGradientModule, torchopt.diff.implicit_diff, MetaSGD, or Optax-style wrapper).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-43320010-3a7d