{"id": "1b4f964b-fdd4-4922-9f79-2a98b4b85b3b", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:44:07.065045", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "32103402-c2cd-4df1-a121-d595f710db3b", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bb6cfaa1-2fc4-419d-be03-2f4edb71e90a", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bb6cfaa1-2fc4-419d-be03-2f4edb71e90a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Deliver initial inspection and plan outline\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n# Initial Inspection & Plan Outline -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\n## 1. Repository & Source Inspection\\n\\n**Workspace state (/workspace).** Only `/workspace/task.json` (375 B) is present. Its embedded `track.md` reads: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No source tree, no cloned TorchOpt, no docs bundle, no `notes/` (I created `notes/initial_inspection.md` to record probes).\\n\\n**Environment probes.**\\n- Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, ~707 GB free; bubblewrap; **network disabled** (curl to pypi.org fails with \\\"Could not resolve host\\\").\\n- `pip` is not on PATH (only the systemd pipe units and a `/lib/python3/dist-packages/pip` source dir were found).\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import X\\\"` resolves to ModuleNotFoundError for: `torch`, `torchopt`, `numpy`, `scipy`.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 6 -iname 'torchopt*'` and `-iname 'torch' -type d` returned zero hits.\\n- No offline dataset mirrors present.\\n\\n**TorchOpt install state.** TorchOpt is **not installed** in this bubblewrap and cannot be installed here (no pip binary reachable, no network). No TorchOpt source/docs are available locally for me to cite from. Therefore every TorchOpt-specific class/function name I propose below is **a known-from-public-docs API surface, not a locally verified one**, and I will flag each such reference with \\\"proposed -- validate against your installed TorchOpt version.\\\"\\n\\n**Locally verified facts (no TorchOpt).** None of the proposed TorchOpt symbols are locally verified. The only things verified here are positive environmental negatives: TorchOpt not in installed packages, TorchOpt not in filesystem search, network not available, pip CLI not available.\\n\\n**Feasibility fallback (so the design is not empty if TorchOpt is not usable).** Two-tier plan the researcher can choose between:\\n- **Tier A -- TorchOpt-native (default if a TorchOpt env is provided later).** Use TorchOpt's differentiable / functional meta-learning interface: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule` / `torchopt.MetaSGD`, the functional inner-loop with stop-gradient for the inner trajectory, and an implicit outer solver. Specific entry points I will iterate on after approval: a `torchopt.diff.implicit_diff`-style wrapper, `MetaGradientModule(meta_net, inner_opt, ...)`, and the conjugate-gradient / Neumann helpers exposed by TorchOpt's differentiable-meta APIs. All flagged as proposed -- to be validated.\\n- **Tier B -- TorchOpt-free, functorch-based (the design I can fully specify without source access).** Same algorithmic story expressed with `torch.func` (functional call + `grad` + `vmap`), the standard implicit-grad identity `d theta-star / d phi = (I - alpha * d^2 L_inner / d theta^2)^{-1} * alpha * d^2 L_inner / d theta d phi` solved by either truncated Neumann series (K terms) or conjugate gradient with Tikhonov damping `lambda * I`. Mechanically equivalent to what TorchOpt's implicit wrapper hides. The experiment harness will be structured so that swapping Tier B's hand-written solver for TorchOpt's wrapper later is a one-file change behind a single `meta_solver.py` interface. Because no TorchOpt is installed and the rule says I must not invent unsupported APIs, Tier B is what I would specify concretely; Tier A's TorchOpt-specific call sites are kept as labeled placeholders awaiting researcher confirmation.\\n\\n## 2. Proposed Experimental Design Outline (high-level, NOT yet approved)\\n\\n**Research question (restated operationally).** Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt produce meta-gradients that (a) match the analytic / second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance epsilon, (b) train a meta-learner to comparable or better held-out accuracy than explicit (unrolled) MAML and FOMAML given a fixed inner-step budget, and (c) do so with lower wall-clock per meta-iteration and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?\\n\\n**Candidate implicit-diff approaches to compare (k = 5 arms).**\\n1. `EXPLICIT-MAML` (unroll-K, retain_graph; Finn et al. 2017) -- upper-bound on accuracy, baseline for cost.\\n2. `FOMAML` (unroll-K, first-order, detach inner grads) -- first-order upper-bound baseline.\\n3. `IMPLICIT-NEUMANN-K` -- implicit-diff via truncated Neumann series with depth K in {3, 5, 10}.\\n4. `IMPLICIT-CG-T` -- implicit-diff via conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system, outer iterations T in {5, 10, 20}.\\n5. `IMPLICIT-EXACT` -- when feasible on the small models, solve the linear system exactly as a feasibility / oracle reference (smallest toy only; not run on the image tasks).\\n\\nAll implicit arms share the same inner loss L_inner, inner optimizer (SGD with inner LR alpha), inner-step budget (K_init steps used only to reach the regime where the fixed-point approximation is realistic; the meta-gradient itself comes from the implicit solve, not from the unrolling), and meta-batch size.\\n\\n**Baselines.** EXPLICIT-MAML (reference for gradient fidelity), FOMAML (reference for first-order cost), and an analytic gradient on the toy regression (closed-form Jacobian of the 1-step linear inner loop) as an oracle for grad-match metrics.\\n\\n**Task suite (CPU-feasible; no network).**\\n- Toy linear regression (`y = w-star . x + eps`, K=1 inner step): provides a closed-form Jacobian, lets us measure the relative L2 norm of the difference between g_implicit and g_analytic directly. Synthetic, instant.\\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 protocol: amplitude in [0.1, 5.0], phase in [0, pi], K_shot = 5, K_query = 10, 4-gradient-update inner steps). Synthetic.\\n- Sinusoid classification / Omniglot N-way K-shot is preferred if an offline mirror is supplied; otherwise a synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot benchmark (mixture of Gaussians in R^d, controllable inter-class margin) is used so the experiment is reproducible without downloads.\\n\\n**Network.** MLP with 2 hidden layers of 40 units and ReLU for the regression arms; a 4-layer ConvNet (matching the standard MiniImageNet MAML conv) for the classification arm. All small enough to fit in CPU memory with batch size up to 4 tasks of size 25.\\n\\n**Inner-loop optimizer.** `torchopt.MetaSGD` is the default candidate (per-parameter inner LR), with `torch.optim.SGD` as the fallback if the TorchOpt API is unavailable in the target env. Inner LR alpha in {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}; outer LR beta = 0.001 (Adam for the meta-optimizer).\\n\\n**Outer-loop / implicit-solver hyperparameters.**\\n- Damping lambda in {0.0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} applied as `(H + lambda I)` to keep the system well-conditioned (Lorraine et al. 2020, Rajeswaran et al. 2019, iMAML).\\n- Neumann depth K in {3, 5, 10}; CG iterations T in {5, 10, 20}; CG tolerance 1e-5.\\n- Hessian-vector products via double-backward `torch.autograd.grad` (or functorch's `jvp` / `vjp`), avoiding ever materializing the full Hessian.\\n\\n**Inner-adaptation steps.** K_inner in {1, 5, 10}; sweeping K_inner lets us check the claim \\\"implicit-diff decouples K_inner from memory cost.\\\" (K_inner is the number of inner steps at inference / adaptation; for the implicit arms, the meta-gradient is independent of K_inner at the fixed point.)\\n\\n**Learning rates / batch sizes.** Meta batch (tasks per step) in {2, 8, 32} for the regression arms; in {2, 4} for the conv arm. Outer LR beta = 0.001.\\n\\n**Meta-iterations & seeds.** 5,000 meta-iterations on the toy, 10,000 on sine, 20,000 on classification. Seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm; mean and std reported.\\n\\n**Metrics.**\\n- Gradient fidelity: relative L2 norm of `(g_implicit(lambda, K) - g_explicit)` divided by norm of g_explicit on the toy (where g_explicit is the unrolled-K second-order gradient, and g_analytic is the closed form for K=1).\\n- Solver convergence: CG residual L2 norm per outer iter; Neumann tail.\\n- Meta-test performance: few-shot accuracy / MSE on a held-out 600-task test split, with confidence intervals from the 3 seeds.\\n- Inner-loss trajectories: mean and std of L_query vs. inner adaptation step on the test support / query split (checks that the implicit arm can match EXPLICIT-MAML's adapted parameters' loss curve).\\n- Compute cost: wall-clock seconds per meta-iteration; peak resident memory (`tracemalloc` / `resource.getrusage` on CPU); inner-tape memory = bytes held by `retain_graph` graph.\\n- Stability: fraction of iterations where the implicit solve diverged, under each lambda and K.\\n\\n**Analysis plan.**\\n1. Gradient-fidelity table: rows = arm, columns = K_inner; cell = mean gradient-relative error with std across seeds.\\n2. Meta-test curve: learning curves (meta-test acc or MSE vs. meta-iteration) for all 5 arms; log-wall-clock on the x-axis of a second plot to expose speed-accuracy tradeoff.\\n3. Pareto front: per-arm (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory) tuples; check whether any implicit arm dominates EXPLICIT-MAML on at least one axis.\\n4. Ablations over lambda and K (Neumann depth) / T (CG iters): verify the lambda not-equal-zero improves conditioning and that K=5 or T=10 is enough given alpha below 1 over the Lipschitz constant of the inner Hessian.\\n5. Failure-mode log: for each divergence event record (alpha, lambda, K, K_inner) and the linear-system residual at the last outer step -- supports a discussion of when implicit-diff pays off.\\n\\n**Controls.**\\n- Random-seed control (3 seeds per arm).\\n- Same inner-step count across arms within a task tier.\\n- Shared inner-network initialization distribution (deterministic-given-seed).\\n- Data-generation RNG and task-sampling RNG separated so test tasks are held out and unseen.\\n- Sanity probe: with alpha = 0, every implicit arm must return the FOMAML gradient (degenerate control).\\n\\n**Feasibility check (no-env estimate).**\\n- Toy regression + sine-wave both run on CPU in seconds to minutes per arm times seed (tiny networks, K_inner below 10).\\n- Synthetic N-way K-shot classification with the 4-layer ConvNet at meta-batch 4 tasks times 25 examples is the heavy arm; estimated minutes per meta-iteration on the 24-core box, so 20K iterations times 5 arms times 3 seeds is the dominant cost -- multi-hour, but well within an overnight CPU budget. Cannot be run in this bubblewrap (no torch) but the design size is CPU-realistic.\\n- Memory: implicit arm stores only O(params) extra activations versus EXPLICIT-MAML's O(params * K_inner); this is asserted in the design and is itself a result to be measured.\\n\\n## 3. Assumptions & Uncertainties\\n\\n**A. Blocking questions (must be answered before I write the final design).**\\n1. Which TorchOpt version / which entry point is intended? I have not found TorchOpt locally; I need the researcher to confirm the intended interface among: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule`, a `torchopt.diff.implicit_diff`-style wrapper, `torchopt.MetaSGD` plus manual CG / Neumann, or an Optax-style `optax.implicit_diff` (if the Optax-TorchOpt wrapper is the intended surface). My plan above includes a TorchOpt-free Tier B that maps 1-to-1 onto whatever TorchOpt helper the researcher confirms, so the rest of the design is robust to this answer.\\n2. What is the target of differentiation? (a) Only the inner-loop initialization theta-zero (classical iMAML), (b) per-parameter inner learning rates and initialization (MetaSGD-style), or (c) outer / hyperparameters like regularization weight? My outline assumes (a) with (b) as a secondary arm.\\n3. Compute budget and expected success thresholds. What is the wall-clock ceiling per arm, and what meta-test gap vs. EXPLICIT-MAML is acceptable for declaring the implicit arm successful (for example, within 1 percentage point on classification, within 0.005 MSE on sine, gradient-relative error at most 1e-2 on the toy)? These thresholds belong in the final design.\\n4. Tasks and data. Are offline mirrors of MiniImageNet / CIFAR-FS / Omniglot available, or must I stay synthetic? My fallback is a synthetic Gaussian-mixture few-shot benchmark; if real-task data is available the classification arm should switch to it.\\n5. Tier decision. Should the deliverable specify Tier A (TorchOpt calls literally named, with API names marked as proposed-to-validate) or Tier B (functorch code that is fully specifiable without source access and is wrapped so a TorchOpt call can be slotted in once confirmed)?\\n\\n**B. Non-blocking defaults I will choose myself with stated rationale (overridable at approval time).**\\n- Inner optimizer is SGD with per-parameter LR (MetaSGD where available, plain SGD fallback).\\n- Damping lambda swept over {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} with 1e-2 as default (matches iMAML's reported regime).\\n- Neumann depth K = 5; CG iters T = 10 as defaults (typical neighboring-paper defaults).\\n- Meta-optimizer is Adam, beta = 0.001 (standard).\\n- 3 seeds, meta-test set of 600 held-out tasks.\\n- All small enough for CPU (no GPU dependency in the design), since GPU is disabled.\\n- Gradient-fidelity metric on the toy uses the K=1 closed form; on other tasks it uses unrolled-K second-order as the reference.\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture few-shot if no real dataset mirror is supplied.\\n\\n**C. Known gotchas I will address in the final design (not blockers, listed for transparency).**\\n- `retain_graph=True` is required for the EXPLICIT arm; memory cost will be reported separately from grad-error so it does not contaminate the gradient-fidelity metric.\\n- For deeper inner loops, the Neumann series only converges if `alpha * Hessian-Lipschitz` is below 1; I will verify this condition empirically before drawing conclusions about K.\\n- Truncated Neumann + CG are not the only options (there is also the Gauss-Newton / BFGS implicit-meta family); I include them only if there is room, as a sensitivity arm, after the primary five-arm comparison is locked in.\\n\\n**Status.** Awaiting researcher approval on items 1-5 in section A before drafting the full design (rationale, controls, full hyperparameter table, analysis plan with statistical tests, and the concrete Tier-A vs. Tier-B implementation strategy). No experiment has been run; no outcomes are claimed.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"Environment probe output (Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, ~707 GB free, no pip on PATH, no torch / torchopt / numpy / scipy, network disabled via curl to pypi.org returning \\\"Could not resolve host\\\", find / surfaced no torch or torchopt directories). /workspace/task.json content confirms a design-only scope; its embedded documents.track.md says \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Scope notes saved to /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md for the researcher's reference. No locally verified TorchOpt APIs: every TorchOpt symbol named in the plan is proposed-from-public-docs and explicitly flagged for researcher confirmation against the actual installed TorchOpt version.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"TorchOpt is NOT installed in this bubblewrap and cannot be installed (no pip, no network); therefore every TorchOpt API name in the plan is a public-docs-based proposal, not a locally verified fact.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Tier B (functorch-based, TorchOpt-free) is a valid placeholder that maps 1-to-1 onto Tier A; swapping in the real TorchOpt call later is a single-file change behind a meta_solver.py interface.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Compute is CPU-only with 24 cores and is feasible for the proposed small-network arms (MLP-40 / ConvNet-4-layer) within a multi-hour overnight budget; GPU is not used.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"No offline dataset mirrors of MiniImageNet / CIFAR-FS / Omniglot are present, so the classification arm will use a synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot benchmark unless the researcher supplies data.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Default inner optimizer is SGD with per-parameter LR (torchopt.MetaSGD candidate, torch.optim.SGD fallback); meta-optimizer is Adam, beta = 0.001.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"First-order implicit (for example setting damping high or skipping the Gauss-Newton inversion) is acceptable as a within-arm condition; second-order implicit is the primary arm but not a hard requirement.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Implicit gradient target is the inner-loop initialization theta-zero (iMAML setting), with MetaSGD-style per-parameter inner LR as a secondary arm.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"BLOCKING: which TorchOpt version / entry point is intended (MetaGradientModule, torchopt.diff.implicit_diff, MetaSGD, or Optax-style wrapper).</item>\",\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: whether real few-shot datasets can be supplied offline, or whether the synthetic Gaussian-mixture benchmark is acceptable.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: target of differentiation (initialization theta-zero vs. per-parameter inner LR vs. other meta-parameters).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: which Tier (A: TorchOpt-literal calls, B: functorch with TorchOpt slot) the researcher wants the deliverable to specify concretely.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"BLOCKING: compute budget ceiling per arm and the empirical success thresholds (allowed gap to EXPLICIT-MAML on accuracy or MSE, allowed gradient-relative error).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Minor: second-order vs. first-order implicit acceptability, Neumann depth default (5 vs. 10), CG iters default (10 vs. 20), damping lambda default sweep grid, number of seeds (3 vs. 5).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e40cf2d0-cd4d-4251-8795-83cb5e260489", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 26}
