{"id": "afed5af4-9737-41c7-8c61-8af847b365af", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:44:31.903963", "source": "user", "parent_id": "0471b0d3-d3de-4bec-9d2e-af1bf97f544b", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The handoff I received is a schema probe ('Test answer - schema probe') rather than the three-section initial inspection I requested. Before I can review or approve any approach, I need the actual deliverable. Please re-emit the initial_inspection reply with these three sections only, concrete and grounded in whatever you can verify locally (no network): (1) Repository and source inspection — concretely list what is actually present under /workspace (files, any existing meta-learning code), report whether TorchOpt (or any meta-learning library) is installed in this system Python; if not installed and network is disabled, state that explicitly and propose a design that is feasible under the stated environment (system Python, no network, no GPU). If TorchOpt source/docs are present locally, summarize the relevant API surface for implicit differentiation (functional/differentiable/implicit-meta interfaces, HVP/CG/Neumann helpers). (2) Proposed experimental design outline (high-level, pending approval) — restate the question operationally, name the candidate TorchOpt implicit-differentiation approach(es) with the specific TorchOpt modules/classes/algorithms you intend to use (justify from local evidence, otherwise flag as assumption), the explicit/forward-mode baselines you would compare against, the task suite (default to synthetic/toy if no offline datasets are available, since network is disabled), meta-training algorithm and inner-loop optimizer, outer-loop update rule and implicit-solver hyperparameters (CG iterations/damping or Neumann depth), inner-adaptation step count, learning rates/batch sizes, meta-iteration count, seeds, and metrics (inner-loss trajectories, meta-test performance, iteration cost, memory). (3) Assumptions and uncertainties — enumerate unresolved setup questions (TorchOpt version and which implicit-differentiation interface is targeted; whether differentiation targets inner-loop initialization vs. meta-parameters; first-order implicit vs. full second-order implicit; regression vs. classification or both; compute budget; success thresholds) and tag each as 'blocks approval' or 'minor default I can choose with stated rationale'. Save any supporting notes under /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md if useful. Do not start writing the final design yet; I will approve the outline before main work proceeds.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 30}
{"id": "4952bbb2-f772-4a52-bdec-c45eab627acf", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:45:25.304629", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "afed5af4-9737-41c7-8c61-8af847b365af", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-552c49d8-cbc2-47d5-9ce9-da3d1e575a82", "tool_call": {"id": "call-552c49d8-cbc2-47d5-9ce9-da3d1e575a82", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Re-emit three-section plan after schema test\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n# Initial Inspection & Plan Outline -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\n## 1. Repository & Source Inspection\\n\\n**Workspace state.** /workspace contains a single file, /workspace/task.json (375 bytes). Its embedded documents.track.md reads \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" There is no source tree, no cloned TorchOpt repository, no documentation bundle, and no pre-existing meta-learning code or scripts anywhere under /workspace. I created /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md to record the probe output.\\n\\n**Environment probes.**\\n- Python is CPython 3.12.3 on Linux x86_64 (kernel 7.0.0-38-generic, Ubuntu 24.04 host).\\n- 24 logical CPUs (nproc); roughly 707 GB free disk on /workspace.\\n- pip is not on the PATH (the which builtin is not present; find only surfaces systemd pipe units and /lib/python3/dist-packages/pip, which is a source directory rather than a runnable CLI).\\n- Network is disabled; curl to pypi.org returns Could not resolve host.\\n- python3 -c \\\"import X\\\" triggers ModuleNotFoundError for torch, torchopt, numpy, scipy.\\n- find / -maxdepth 6 -iname \\\"torchopt*\\\" and -iname \\\"torch\\\" -type d returned zero hits. No offline dataset mirrors are present.\\n\\n**TorchOpt install state.** TorchOpt is not installed in this bubblewrap. It cannot be installed here because no pip CLI is reachable and the network is disabled. No TorchOpt source, no TorchOpt documentation, and no TorchOpt-distributed examples are present locally. None of the TorchOpt symbols proposed below are locally verified; they are based on what is publicly documented for TorchOpt and are flagged throughout as proposed-to-validate.\\n\\n**Locally verified facts.** Only environmental negatives: TorchOpt not in site-packages; TorchOpt not on disk; pip CLI not available; network not available; no torch, numpy, scipy installed; no datasets present; no pre-existing meta-learning code under /workspace.\\n\\n**Feasibility fallback.** The experiment design is feasible under the stated environment in two tiers, which the researcher can choose between at approval time.\\n- Tier A (TorchOpt-native, the intended deliverable if a TorchOpt-equipped Python is provided later): use the TorchOpt differentiable and functional meta-learning API. Proposed entry points (all flagged as proposed -- validate against the user's installed TorchOpt version): torchopt.MetaGradientModule, torchopt.MetaSGD, a torchopt.diff.implicit_diff-style functional wrapper, and the conjugate-gradient and Neumann helpers exposed by TorchOpt's differentiable-meta module.\\n- Tier B (TorchOpt-free, functorch-based, fully specifiable without TorchOpt source access): implement the same algorithm with torch.func (functional call plus grad plus vmap) and the standard implicit-grad identity d_theta-star / d_phi = inverse(I minus alpha times second-derivative-of-L_inner) times alpha times mixed-derivative-of-L_inner, solved by truncated Neumann series of depth K or by conjugate gradient with Tikhonov damping lambda times I. The experiment harness factors the implicit solver behind a single meta_solver.py interface so that swapping Tier B for Tier A later is a one-file change. Because TorchOpt is not installed and the rule prohibits inventing unsupported APIs, Tier B is the tier I would specify concretely in any code-level detail; Tier A's TorchOpt call sites are kept as labeled placeholders awaiting researcher confirmation.\\n\\n## 2. Proposed Experimental Design Outline (high-level, pending approval)\\n\\n**Research question, restated operationally.** Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) produce meta-gradients that match the analytic or second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance epsilon, (b) train a meta-learner to held-out accuracy or loss comparable to or better than explicit MAML and FOMAML given a fixed inner-step budget, and (c) do so with lower per-meta-iteration wall-clock time and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?\\n\\n**Candidate implicit-diff approaches (5 arms).**\\n1. EXPLICIT-MAML: unroll K inner steps with retain_graph on. Finn et al. 2017 baseline; reference for gradient fidelity and upper bound for accuracy.\\n2. FOMAML: unroll K inner steps with detached inner gradients. First-order upper-bound baseline.\\n3. IMPLICIT-NEUMANN-K: implicit-diff via truncated Neumann series with depth K drawn from {3, 5, 10}.\\n4. IMPLICIT-CG-T: implicit-diff via conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system, outer iterations T drawn from {5, 10, 20}, tolerance 1e-5.\\n5. IMPLICIT-EXACT: solve the damped linear system exactly as a feasibility or oracle reference, only on the smallest toy task.\\n\\nAll implicit arms share the same inner loss L_inner, the same inner optimizer (SGD with inner LR alpha), the same inner-loop initialization, and the same meta-batch size. The implicit arms compute the meta-gradient through the fixed-point solve rather than by unrolling.\\n\\n**Baselines.** EXPLICIT-MAML (gradient fidelity reference), FOMAML (first-order cost reference), and on the toy task a closed-form analytic meta-gradient for the 1-step linear inner loop as an oracle for gradient-matching metrics.\\n\\n**Task suite.** Defaults to synthetic since the network is disabled and no offline dataset mirrors are present.\\n- Toy linear regression (y = w-star . x + eps, K = 1 inner step): supplies the closed-form Jacobian and supports direct gradient-fidelity measurement.\\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 protocol, amplitude in [0.1, 5.0], phase in [0, pi], K_shot = 5, K_query = 10, 4 inner gradient-update steps).\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot classification as the offline default for the image-style arm; with optional switch to MiniImageNet or CIFAR-FS if the researcher supplies mirrored data.\\n\\n**Network.** MLP with 2 hidden layers of 40 units and ReLU for the regression arms. A 4-layer ConvNet matching the standard MiniImageNet MAML conv for the classification arm. Small enough for CPU batch sizes up to 4 tasks of 25 examples each.\\n\\n**Inner-loop optimizer.** torchopt.MetaSGD (per-parameter inner LR) as the default candidate, with torch.optim.SGD as a fallback if the TorchOpt API is unavailable in the target environment. Inner LR alpha drawn from {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}.\\n\\n**Meta-training algorithm and outer-loop update rule.** Meta-optimizer is Adam with outer LR beta = 0.001. Outer update is the standard MAML outer step applied to the meta-parameters (theta-zero, and additionally the per-parameter inner LR for the MetaSGD secondary arm).\\n\\n**Implicit-solver hyperparameters.**\\n- Damping lambda drawn from {0.0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}, applied as H plus lambda times I. Default 1e-2. References: Lorraine et al. 2020, Rajeswaran et al. 2019, iMAML.\\n- Neumann depth K in {3, 5, 10}, default 5.\\n- CG iterations T in {5, 10, 20}, default 10; CG tolerance 1e-5.\\n- Hessian-vector products via double-backward torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp and vjp; never materialize the full Hessian.\\n\\n**Inner-adaptation step count.** K_inner drawn from {1, 5, 10}. Sweeping K_inner explicitly tests the implicit-diff claim that meta-gradient cost is independent of K_inner.\\n\\n**Learning rates and batch sizes.** Meta batch (tasks per step) in {2, 8, 32} for the regression arms; in {2, 4} for the conv arm. Outer LR beta = 0.001 throughout.\\n\\n**Meta-iteration count and seeds.** 5000 meta-iterations on toy; 10000 on sine; 20000 on classification. Seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm; report mean and std.\\n\\n**Metrics.**\\n- Inner-loss trajectories: L_query versus inner adaptation step on a held-out support or query split, mean and std across seeds; confirms the implicit arm matches EXPLICIT-MAML's adapted-parameter loss curve.\\n- Meta-test performance: few-shot accuracy (classification) or MSE (regression) on a 600-task held-out test split, with confidence intervals from 3 seeds.\\n- Per-iteration wall-clock seconds, measured with time.perf_counter around the inner and outer step chunks separately.\\n- Peak resident memory via resource.getrusage on CPU; inner-tape memory = bytes held by the retain_graph graph (parameter count times K_inner times dtype size).\\n- Stability: fraction of iterations where the implicit solve diverged under each lambda and K, plus per-event log of (alpha, lambda, K, K_inner, last residual).\\n\\n**Analysis plan.**\\n1. Gradient-fidelity table: rows = arm, columns = K_inner; cell = mean gradient-relative L2 error with std across seeds.\\n2. Meta-test learning curves for all 5 arms; a second plot uses log wall-clock on the x-axis to expose speed/accuracy tradeoffs.\\n3. Pareto front over (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory); identify any implicit arm that dominates EXPLICIT-MAML on at least one axis.\\n4. Ablations over lambda, Neumann depth K, and CG iters T; verify that nonzero lambda improves conditioning and K=5 or T=10 is sufficient for the operating regime.\\n5. Failure-mode log for divergence events, with conditioning diagnostics from the last solve.\\n\\n**Controls.**\\n- 3 seeds per arm.\\n- Same inner-step count across arms within each task tier.\\n- Shared inner-network initialization distribution, deterministic given seed.\\n- Separate PRNG streams for data generation and task sampling so test tasks are unseen.\\n- Sanity probe: with alpha = 0 every implicit arm must return the FOMAML gradient.\\n\\n**Feasibility check (no-env estimate).**\\n- Toy regression and sine-wave run on CPU in seconds to minutes per arm times seed (tiny networks, K_inner up to 10).\\n- Synthetic N-way K-shot ConvNet at meta-batch 4 tasks times 25 examples is the heavy arm; estimated minutes per iteration on the 24-core box; 20000 iterations times 5 arms times 3 seeds is multi-hour, but within an overnight CPU budget. Cannot be executed in this bubblewrap (no torch) but the design size is CPU-realistic.\\n- Memory: implicit arm should hold O(params) extra activations versus EXPLICIT-MAML's O(params times K_inner); asserted in the design and itself a measurement.\\n\\n## 3. Assumptions & Uncertainties\\n\\n**A. Setup questions tagged blocks-approval.**\\n1. Which TorchOpt version and which implicit-differentiation entry point is intended: torchopt.MetaGradientModule, a torchopt.diff.implicit_diff-style wrapper, torchopt.MetaSGD plus manual CG or Neumann, or an Optax-style optax.implicit_diff. blocks approval.\\n2. What is the target of differentiation: (a) the inner-loop initialization theta-zero only (classical iMAML), (b) per-parameter inner learning rates and initialization (MetaSGD-style), or (c) outer hyperparameters such as regularization weight. blocks approval.\\n3. Whether second-order implicit (full Gauss-Newton solve) is required or first-order implicit (heavy damping, no inversion) is acceptable. blocks approval.\\n4. Whether the benchmark must include classification or only regression (or both). blocks approval for picking the task suite.\\n5. Compute budget ceiling per arm and the success thresholds versus EXPLICIT-MAML on accuracy or MSE and on gradient-relative error. blocks approval.\\n6. Are offline dataset mirrors of MiniImageNet, CIFAR-FS, or Omniglot available, or must the design stay on synthetic tasks (this design assumes synthetic Gaussian-mixture as the classification default unless the researcher says otherwise). blocks approval.\\n\\n**B. Setup questions tagged minor-default-with-rationale (I will choose unless the researcher overrides).**\\n- Inner optimizer is SGD with per-parameter LR (MetaSGD where available, plain SGD fallback). Maintains parity with the MAML family.\\n- Damping lambda swept over {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} with 1e-2 as default; matches the iMAML operating regime in the literature.\\n- Neumann depth K = 5 and CG iters T = 10 as defaults; typical neighboring-paper values.\\n- Meta-optimizer is Adam, outer LR beta = 0.001; standard in the MAML literature.\\n- 3 seeds per arm, 600-task held-out meta-test split; trades compute for confidence-interval width.\\n- Designed for CPU, no GPU dependency, since the environment reports GPU disabled.\\n- Gradient fidelity on the toy task uses the K=1 closed-form analytic gradient; on other tasks it uses unrolled-K second-order MAML as the reference, with the limitation acknowledged.\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot as the offline classification default.\\n\\n**C. Known gotchas I will address in the final design (not blockers, listed for transparency).**\\n- EXPLICIT-MAML requires retain_graph=True; I will report its memory cost separately so it does not contaminate the gradient-fidelity metric.\\n- The Neumann series converges only if alpha times the Hessian operator norm is strictly below 1; the design verifies this empirically before drawing conclusions about K.\\n- Gauss-Newton and BFGS implicit-meta variants are sensitivity arms, included after the primary five-arm comparison is locked.\\n\\n**Status.** Awaiting researcher approval on the six items in section A before drafting the full design (rationale, controls, full hyperparameter table, analysis plan with statistical tests, and the concrete Tier-A vs. Tier-B implementation strategy). No experiment has been run; no outcomes are claimed.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"Environment probe output: Python 3.12.3 on Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, ~707 GB free on /workspace. pip is not on PATH (which not present; only a non-runnable pip source dir found). Network is disabled (curl to pypi.org returns Could not resolve host). python3 -c \\\"import X\\\" yields ModuleNotFoundError for torch, torchopt, numpy, scipy. find / -maxdepth 6 -iname \\\"torchopt*\\\" and -iname \\\"torch\\\" -type d returned zero hits. /workspace/task.json is the only file; its embedded documents.track.md confirms a design-only scope. Scope notes are saved to /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md. No TorchOpt symbols are locally verified; every TorchOpt symbol proposed in this plan is flagged as proposed-to-validate and is grounded in publicly documented TorchOpt behavior rather than local source.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Implicit gradient target defaults to inner-loop initialization theta-zero (iMAML), with MetaSGD per-parameter inner LR as a secondary arm.</item>\",\"$text\":\"First-order implicit (e.g., heavy damping, no inversion) is acceptable as a within-arm condition; second-order implicit is the primary arm.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Default inner optimizer is SGD with per-parameter LR (MetaSGD preferred, plain SGD fallback); meta-optimizer is Adam, outer LR beta = 0.001.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"No offline dataset mirrors of MiniImageNet, CIFAR-FS, or Omniglot are present, so the classification arm defaults to a synthetic Gaussian-mixture unless the researcher supplies data.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Compute is CPU-only with 24 cores and is feasible for the proposed small-network arms within a multi-hour overnight budget.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Tier B (TorchOpt-free functorch implementation) is a valid placeholder that maps 1-to-1 onto Tier A TorchOpt-native calls and can be swapped in later via meta_solver.py.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"TorchOpt is NOT installed in this bubblewrap and cannot be installed (no pip, no network).</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"minor default: Neumann depth default 5, CG iters default 10, damping default 1e-2, seeds = 3, meta-test set size = 600.</item>\",\"$text\":\"blocks approval: offline real-task dataset availability (default if absent: synthetic Gaussian-mixture classification).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"blocks approval: compute budget per arm and success thresholds vs EXPLICIT-MAML.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"blocks approval: classification required, regression only, or both.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"blocks approval: second-order implicit required vs first-order implicit acceptable.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"blocks approval: target of differentiation (init theta-zero vs per-parameter inner LR vs outer hyperparameters).</item>\"},\"$text\":\"blocks approval: which TorchOpt version / entry point is intended (MetaGradientModule, diff.implicit_diff, MetaSGD, or Optax-style wrapper).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4e206e3e-6936-45e3-a8c1-f2bc6f327b13", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 31}
