{"id": "7908e12a-4522-43dd-8ae7-4b53416547d4", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:46:42.089556", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9d67d871-d8bf-4df8-a26d-0e2fa5f50e49", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "\n# Initial Inspection and Plan Outline for Implicit Differentiation in Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\n\n## 1. Repository and source inspection\n\nWorkspace contents. Under /workspace there is exactly one file: /workspace/task.json of 375 bytes. Its embedded documents.track.md states that this is a design-only task and that repositories and compute are not required. No source tree exists. No TorchOpt repository clone exists. No documentation bundle exists. No pre-existing meta-learning code or scripts exist anywhere under /workspace. I created /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md during the inspection pass to record findings.\n\nEnvironment probes. Python is CPython 3.12.3 on Linux x86_64 with 24 logical CPUs and about 707 GB free on /workspace. pip is not present on PATH and a recursive find only surfaced the systemd pipe units and the source directory /lib/python3/dist-packages/pip which is not a runnable CLI. Network is disabled because curl to pypi.org returns a DNS Could not resolve host error. python3 -c \"import X\" yields ModuleNotFoundError for torch for torchopt for numpy and for scipy. find -maxdepth 6 found no torchopt directory and no torch directory anywhere on disk. No offline dataset mirrors are present.\n\nTorchOpt install state. TorchOpt is not installed in this system Python. TorchOpt cannot be installed here because no pip CLI is reachable and the network is disabled. No TorchOpt source files and no TorchOpt documentation are present locally. None of the TorchOpt symbols proposed below are locally verified facts. They are based on publicly documented TorchOpt behavior and are explicitly labeled as proposed-to-validate or assumption throughout.\n\nLocally verified facts. The only verified facts are the environmental negatives listed above. Specifically nothing has been locally verified about TorchOpt itself.\n\nRelevant TorchOpt API surface (proposed-to-validate only — not source-verified). Because no TorchOpt source is available locally the items below are explicitly unverified proposals drawn from publicly documented behavior of TorchOpt. They will be revised against the user's actual installed TorchOpt version after approval.\n- Per-parameter inner learning rates: torchopt.MetaSGD\n- Differentiable meta-learning wrapper: torchopt.MetaGradientModule with a meta_net object exposing meta_parameters and a differentiable inner-loop solver\n- Functional inner-loop with stop-gradient on the trajectory and implicit-grad for the meta step: variants of torchopt.diff.implicit_diff over functional.call and the inner optimizer\n- Outer-loop conjugate-gradient or Neumann helpers for the damped Gauss-Newton system: helpers exposed by TorchOpt's differentiable meta-learning module (exact names to be confirmed after approval)\n- Hessian-vector products via double backward torch.autograd.grad or via functorch jvp and vjp — these are PyTorch primitives rather than TorchOpt-specific but are how the implicit solve evaluates the matrix-vector products\n\nFallback path when TorchOpt is not usable. If TorchOpt remains unavailable the experiment harness factors the implicit solver behind a single meta_solver.py module and ships a TorchOpt-free implementation using torch.func and the canonical implicit-grad identity d_theta_star over d_phi = inverse (I minus alpha times second derivative of L inner) times alpha times mixed derivative of L inner. The identity is solved by truncated Neumann series with depth K or by conjugate gradient with Tikhonov damping lambda times I. Tier A and Tier B are mechanically equivalent and the only change required to migrate is replacing the body of meta_solver.py.\n\n## 2. Proposed experimental design outline (high-level pending approval)\n\nResearch question restated operationally. Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) produce meta-gradients that match the analytic or second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance epsilon (b) train a meta-learner to held-out accuracy or loss comparable to or better than explicit MAML and FOMAML for a fixed inner-step budget and (c) do so with lower per-meta-iteration wall-clock time and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?\n\nCandidate TorchOpt implicit-differentiation approach (5 arms).\n1. EXPLICIT-MAML: unroll K inner steps with retain_graph on. Finn et al. 2017 baseline. Reference for gradient fidelity and an accuracy upper bound.\n2. FOMAML: unroll K inner steps with detached inner gradients. First-order accuracy reference and a lower-bound cost baseline.\n3. IMPLICIT-NEUMANN-K: implicit-diff via truncated Neumann series with depth K drawn from {3, 5, 10}. Implemented through the proposed TorchOpt differentiable meta interface.\n4. IMPLICIT-CG-T: implicit-diff via conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system with outer iterations T drawn from {5, 10, 20} and tolerance 1e-5. Implemented through the proposed TorchOpt differentiable meta interface.\n5. IMPLICIT-EXACT: solve the damped linear system exactly as a feasibility and oracle reference. Run only on the smallest toy task where direct factorization is cheap.\n\nAll implicit arms share the same inner loss L_inner the same inner optimizer the same inner initialization distribution and the same meta-batch size. The implicit arms compute the meta-gradient through the fixed-point solve rather than by unrolling the inner trajectory.\n\nBaselines. Explicit MAML for gradient fidelity. FOMAML for first-order cost. REPTILE included as a non-unrolled first-order baseline that performs no second-order correction at all. Additionally on the toy linear regression task a closed-form analytic meta-gradient for the 1-step linear inner loop is computed as an oracle gradient for direct gradient-matching. On the synthetic regression arm the closed-form analytic gradient is also an oracle.\n\nTask suite. Defaults to synthetic because the network is disabled and no offline dataset mirrors are present.\n- Toy linear regression y equals w_star dot x plus epsilon with K equals 1 inner step. Closes the analytic Jacobian and supports direct gradient-fidelity measurement.\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression using the Finn et al. 2017 protocol with amplitude in [0.1, 5.0] and phase in [0, pi] and K_shot equals 5 and K_query equals 10 and 4 inner gradient updates.\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot classification as the offline default image-style task. MiniImageNet and CIFAR-FS are optional substitutions if the researcher supplies mirrored data.\n\nNetwork. MLP with 2 hidden layers of 40 units and ReLU for the regression arms. A 4-layer ConvNet matching the standard MiniImageNet MAML conv for the classification arm. Small enough for CPU batch sizes up to 4 tasks of 25 examples each.\n\nInner-loop optimizer. SGD with per-parameter inner LR via the proposed torchopt.MetaSGD with plain torch.optim.SGD as the fallback if TorchOpt is unavailable. Inner LR alpha drawn from {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}.\n\nMeta-training algorithm and outer-loop update rule. Meta-optimizer is Adam with outer LR beta equals 0.001. Outer update is the standard MAML outer step applied to the meta-parameters. For the MetaSGD secondary arm the outer step also updates the per-parameter inner LR.\n\nImplicit-solver hyperparameters. Damping lambda drawn from {0.0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} applied as H plus lambda times I with default 1e-2 in line with iMAML. Neumann depth K drawn from {3, 5, 10} with default 5. CG iterations T drawn from {5, 10, 20} with default 10 and CG tolerance 1e-5. Hessian-vector products via double backward torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp and vjp. The full Hessian is never materialized.\n\nInner-adaptation step count. K_inner drawn from {1, 5, 10}. The sweep directly tests the implicit-diff claim that meta-gradient cost is independent of K_inner.\n\nLearning rates and batch sizes. Outer LR beta equals 0.001 throughout. Meta batch (tasks per step) drawn from {2, 8, 32} for the regression arms and from {2, 4} for the conv arm.\n\nMeta-iteration count and seeds. 5000 meta-iterations on the toy task. 10000 on the sine task. 20000 on the classification task. Seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm. Report mean and std and 95 percent confidence intervals on the meta-test metrics.\n\nMetrics.\n- Inner-loss trajectories: L_query versus inner adaptation step on a held-out support and query split. Mean and std across seeds. Verifies that the implicit arm matches EXPLICIT-MAML's adapted-parameter loss curve.\n- Meta-test performance: few-shot accuracy on classification and MSE on regression on a 600-task held-out test split. Confidence intervals computed across seeds.\n- Per-iteration wall-clock seconds measured with time.perf_counter separately for the inner step and the outer step.\n- Peak resident memory in bytes via resource.getrusage on CPU. Inner-tape memory computed as parameter count times K_inner times dtype size in bytes.\n- Stability: fraction of iterations where the implicit solve diverged under each lambda and K. Per-event log recording alpha lambda K K_inner and last residual.\n\nAnalysis plan.\n1. Gradient-fidelity table. Rows are arms and columns are K_inner. Each cell reports mean gradient-relative L2 error with std across seeds.\n2. Meta-test learning curves for all 5 arms. A second plot uses log wall-clock on the x-axis instead of iterations to expose speed and accuracy tradeoffs.\n3. Pareto front over (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory). Identify any implicit arm that dominates EXPLICIT-MAML on at least one axis.\n4. Ablations over lambda Neumann depth K and CG iters T. Verify that nonzero lambda improves conditioning and that K equals 5 or T equals 10 suffices within the operating regime.\n5. Failure-mode log for divergence events. Include conditioning diagnostics from the last solve.\n\nControls. Three seeds per arm. Same inner-step count across arms within each task tier. Shared inner-network initialization distribution deterministic given seed. Separate PRNG streams for data generation and task sampling so the held-out meta-test tasks are unseen. Sanity probe: with alpha equals 0 every implicit arm must return the FOMAML gradient.\n\nFeasibility check. Toy regression and sine-wave run on CPU in seconds to minutes per arm per seed. Synthetic N-way K-shot ConvNet at meta-batch 4 tasks times 25 examples is the heavy arm; estimated minutes per iteration on the 24-core box. 20000 iterations times 5 arms times 3 seeds runs multi-hour but within an overnight CPU budget. The design cannot be executed in this bubblewrap because torch is absent; the design size is nevertheless CPU-realistic. Memory expectation is that the implicit arm holds O(params) extra activations versus EXPLICIT-MAML's O(params times K_inner) and this expectation is treated as a result to be measured.\n\n## 3. Assumptions and uncertainties\n\nBlocks-approval items (must be resolved before I draft the final design).\n- Which TorchOpt version and which implicit-differentiation entry point is intended. Among torchopt.MetaGradientModule torchopt.diff.implicit_diff-style wrapper torchopt.MetaSGD plus manual CG or Neumann or an Optax-style optax.implicit_diff. blocks approval.\n- What is the target of differentiation. (a) the inner-loop initialization theta-zero only classical iMAML or (b) per-parameter inner learning rates and initialization MetaSGD-style or (c) outer hyperparameters such as regularization weight. blocks approval.\n- Second-order implicit versus first-order implicit. Must the design include full Gauss-Newton solve or is first-order implicit acceptable. blocks approval.\n- Task coverage. Classification required or regression only or both. blocks approval for selecting the task suite.\n- Compute and iteration budget ceiling per arm. Also the declared success thresholds versus EXPLICIT-MAML on accuracy or MSE and on gradient-relative error. blocks approval.\n- Offline dataset availability. Mirror of MiniImageNet or CIFAR-FS or Omniglot present or absent. blocks approval.\n\nMinor defaults I will choose myself with stated rationale (overridable at approval time).\n- Inner optimizer is SGD with per-parameter LR (MetaSGD preferred plain SGD fallback). Maintains parity with the MAML family. minor default.\n- Damping lambda swept over {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} with 1e-2 as the default. Matches the iMAML operating regime. minor default.\n- Neumann depth K equals 5 and CG iters T equals 10 as defaults. Typical neighboring-paper values. minor default.\n- Meta-optimizer is Adam with outer LR beta equals 0.001. Standard in the MAML literature. minor default.\n- Three seeds per arm and 600-task held-out meta-test split. Trades compute for confidence-interval width. minor default.\n- Designed for CPU with no GPU dependency because the environment reports GPU disabled. minor default.\n- Gradient fidelity on the toy task uses the K equals 1 closed-form analytic gradient. On other tasks the reference is unrolled-K second-order MAML with the limitation acknowledged. minor default.\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot as the offline classification default. minor default.\n\nKnown gotchas (not blockers transparency list).\n- EXPLICIT-MAML needs retain_graph equals True. Its memory cost is reported separately so it does not pollute the gradient-fidelity metric.\n- The Neumann series converges only if alpha times the Hessian operator norm is strictly below 1. The design verifies this empirically before drawing conclusions about K.\n- Gauss-Newton and BFGS implicit-meta variants are sensitivity arms included only after the primary 5-arm comparison is locked in.\n\nStatus. I am waiting for researcher approval on the 6 blocks-approval items above before I write the full design. No experiment has been run. No numerical outcomes are claimed. Supporting probe notes are saved at /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md.\n", "evidence": "Environment probe output during inspection. Python 3.12.3 on Linux x86_64 with 24 CPUs and ~707 GB free on /workspace. pip is not on PATH (find only surfaced a non-runnable pip source dir). Network is disabled (curl to pypi.org returns Could not resolve host). python3 -c \"import X\" yields ModuleNotFoundError for torch, torchopt, numpy and scipy. find found no torch and no torchopt directories on disk. /workspace contains only /workspace/task.json whose embedded documents.track.md confirms a design-only scope. I saved probe notes to /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md. No TorchOpt symbols are locally verified; every TorchOpt symbol in the plan is flagged as proposed-to-validate or assumption and is grounded in publicly documented TorchOpt behavior rather than local source.", "assumptions": ["default init target is iMAML inner init; MetaSGD secondary", "first-order implicit ok as within-arm baseline condition", "no offline datasets so synthetic Gaussian task default", "Tier B functorch path valid swap-in for Tier A TorchOpt path", "TorchOpt not installed and cannot be installed here", "CPU-only design across all arms and seeds", "inner optimizer SGD with per-parameter LR via MetaSGD or fallback", "damping default 1e-2 sweep range from 1e-3 to 1e-1"], "uncertainties": ["blocks approval: offline dataset availability and substitution choice", "blocks approval: compute budget per arm and success thresholds", "blocks approval: regression only or both regression and classification", "blocks approval: first-order or second-order implicit requirement", "blocks approval: target of differentiation init or per-parameter meta params", "blocks approval: which TorchOpt version and which implicit-diff entry point", "minor default: Neumann depth K and CG iters T sweep grid defaults", "minor default: number of seeds and meta-test split size"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-94e12b9f-6c2e-49ea-9905-1aee5ca2de7f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-94e12b9f-6c2e-49ea-9905-1aee5ca2de7f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit substantive three-section plan outline\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Initial Inspection and Plan Outline for Implicit Differentiation in Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\n## 1. Repository and source inspection\\n\\nWorkspace contents. Under /workspace there is exactly one file: /workspace/task.json of 375 bytes. Its embedded documents.track.md states that this is a design-only task and that repositories and compute are not required. No source tree exists. No TorchOpt repository clone exists. No documentation bundle exists. No pre-existing meta-learning code or scripts exist anywhere under /workspace. I created /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md during the inspection pass to record findings.\\n\\nEnvironment probes. Python is CPython 3.12.3 on Linux x86_64 with 24 logical CPUs and about 707 GB free on /workspace. pip is not present on PATH and a recursive find only surfaced the systemd pipe units and the source directory /lib/python3/dist-packages/pip which is not a runnable CLI. Network is disabled because curl to pypi.org returns a DNS Could not resolve host error. python3 -c \\\"import X\\\" yields ModuleNotFoundError for torch for torchopt for numpy and for scipy. find -maxdepth 6 found no torchopt directory and no torch directory anywhere on disk. No offline dataset mirrors are present.\\n\\nTorchOpt install state. TorchOpt is not installed in this system Python. TorchOpt cannot be installed here because no pip CLI is reachable and the network is disabled. No TorchOpt source files and no TorchOpt documentation are present locally. None of the TorchOpt symbols proposed below are locally verified facts. They are based on publicly documented TorchOpt behavior and are explicitly labeled as proposed-to-validate or assumption throughout.\\n\\nLocally verified facts. The only verified facts are the environmental negatives listed above. Specifically nothing has been locally verified about TorchOpt itself.\\n\\nRelevant TorchOpt API surface (proposed-to-validate only \\u2014 not source-verified). Because no TorchOpt source is available locally the items below are explicitly unverified proposals drawn from publicly documented behavior of TorchOpt. They will be revised against the user's actual installed TorchOpt version after approval.\\n- Per-parameter inner learning rates: torchopt.MetaSGD\\n- Differentiable meta-learning wrapper: torchopt.MetaGradientModule with a meta_net object exposing meta_parameters and a differentiable inner-loop solver\\n- Functional inner-loop with stop-gradient on the trajectory and implicit-grad for the meta step: variants of torchopt.diff.implicit_diff over functional.call and the inner optimizer\\n- Outer-loop conjugate-gradient or Neumann helpers for the damped Gauss-Newton system: helpers exposed by TorchOpt's differentiable meta-learning module (exact names to be confirmed after approval)\\n- Hessian-vector products via double backward torch.autograd.grad or via functorch jvp and vjp \\u2014 these are PyTorch primitives rather than TorchOpt-specific but are how the implicit solve evaluates the matrix-vector products\\n\\nFallback path when TorchOpt is not usable. If TorchOpt remains unavailable the experiment harness factors the implicit solver behind a single meta_solver.py module and ships a TorchOpt-free implementation using torch.func and the canonical implicit-grad identity d_theta_star over d_phi = inverse (I minus alpha times second derivative of L inner) times alpha times mixed derivative of L inner. The identity is solved by truncated Neumann series with depth K or by conjugate gradient with Tikhonov damping lambda times I. Tier A and Tier B are mechanically equivalent and the only change required to migrate is replacing the body of meta_solver.py.\\n\\n## 2. Proposed experimental design outline (high-level pending approval)\\n\\nResearch question restated operationally. Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) produce meta-gradients that match the analytic or second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance epsilon (b) train a meta-learner to held-out accuracy or loss comparable to or better than explicit MAML and FOMAML for a fixed inner-step budget and (c) do so with lower per-meta-iteration wall-clock time and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?\\n\\nCandidate TorchOpt implicit-differentiation approach (5 arms).\\n1. EXPLICIT-MAML: unroll K inner steps with retain_graph on. Finn et al. 2017 baseline. Reference for gradient fidelity and an accuracy upper bound.\\n2. FOMAML: unroll K inner steps with detached inner gradients. First-order accuracy reference and a lower-bound cost baseline.\\n3. IMPLICIT-NEUMANN-K: implicit-diff via truncated Neumann series with depth K drawn from {3, 5, 10}. Implemented through the proposed TorchOpt differentiable meta interface.\\n4. IMPLICIT-CG-T: implicit-diff via conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system with outer iterations T drawn from {5, 10, 20} and tolerance 1e-5. Implemented through the proposed TorchOpt differentiable meta interface.\\n5. IMPLICIT-EXACT: solve the damped linear system exactly as a feasibility and oracle reference. Run only on the smallest toy task where direct factorization is cheap.\\n\\nAll implicit arms share the same inner loss L_inner the same inner optimizer the same inner initialization distribution and the same meta-batch size. The implicit arms compute the meta-gradient through the fixed-point solve rather than by unrolling the inner trajectory.\\n\\nBaselines. Explicit MAML for gradient fidelity. FOMAML for first-order cost. REPTILE included as a non-unrolled first-order baseline that performs no second-order correction at all. Additionally on the toy linear regression task a closed-form analytic meta-gradient for the 1-step linear inner loop is computed as an oracle gradient for direct gradient-matching. On the synthetic regression arm the closed-form analytic gradient is also an oracle.\\n\\nTask suite. Defaults to synthetic because the network is disabled and no offline dataset mirrors are present.\\n- Toy linear regression y equals w_star dot x plus epsilon with K equals 1 inner step. Closes the analytic Jacobian and supports direct gradient-fidelity measurement.\\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression using the Finn et al. 2017 protocol with amplitude in [0.1, 5.0] and phase in [0, pi] and K_shot equals 5 and K_query equals 10 and 4 inner gradient updates.\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot classification as the offline default image-style task. MiniImageNet and CIFAR-FS are optional substitutions if the researcher supplies mirrored data.\\n\\nNetwork. MLP with 2 hidden layers of 40 units and ReLU for the regression arms. A 4-layer ConvNet matching the standard MiniImageNet MAML conv for the classification arm. Small enough for CPU batch sizes up to 4 tasks of 25 examples each.\\n\\nInner-loop optimizer. SGD with per-parameter inner LR via the proposed torchopt.MetaSGD with plain torch.optim.SGD as the fallback if TorchOpt is unavailable. Inner LR alpha drawn from {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}.\\n\\nMeta-training algorithm and outer-loop update rule. Meta-optimizer is Adam with outer LR beta equals 0.001. Outer update is the standard MAML outer step applied to the meta-parameters. For the MetaSGD secondary arm the outer step also updates the per-parameter inner LR.\\n\\nImplicit-solver hyperparameters. Damping lambda drawn from {0.0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} applied as H plus lambda times I with default 1e-2 in line with iMAML. Neumann depth K drawn from {3, 5, 10} with default 5. CG iterations T drawn from {5, 10, 20} with default 10 and CG tolerance 1e-5. Hessian-vector products via double backward torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp and vjp. The full Hessian is never materialized.\\n\\nInner-adaptation step count. K_inner drawn from {1, 5, 10}. The sweep directly tests the implicit-diff claim that meta-gradient cost is independent of K_inner.\\n\\nLearning rates and batch sizes. Outer LR beta equals 0.001 throughout. Meta batch (tasks per step) drawn from {2, 8, 32} for the regression arms and from {2, 4} for the conv arm.\\n\\nMeta-iteration count and seeds. 5000 meta-iterations on the toy task. 10000 on the sine task. 20000 on the classification task. Seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm. Report mean and std and 95 percent confidence intervals on the meta-test metrics.\\n\\nMetrics.\\n- Inner-loss trajectories: L_query versus inner adaptation step on a held-out support and query split. Mean and std across seeds. Verifies that the implicit arm matches EXPLICIT-MAML's adapted-parameter loss curve.\\n- Meta-test performance: few-shot accuracy on classification and MSE on regression on a 600-task held-out test split. Confidence intervals computed across seeds.\\n- Per-iteration wall-clock seconds measured with time.perf_counter separately for the inner step and the outer step.\\n- Peak resident memory in bytes via resource.getrusage on CPU. Inner-tape memory computed as parameter count times K_inner times dtype size in bytes.\\n- Stability: fraction of iterations where the implicit solve diverged under each lambda and K. Per-event log recording alpha lambda K K_inner and last residual.\\n\\nAnalysis plan.\\n1. Gradient-fidelity table. Rows are arms and columns are K_inner. Each cell reports mean gradient-relative L2 error with std across seeds.\\n2. Meta-test learning curves for all 5 arms. A second plot uses log wall-clock on the x-axis instead of iterations to expose speed and accuracy tradeoffs.\\n3. Pareto front over (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory). Identify any implicit arm that dominates EXPLICIT-MAML on at least one axis.\\n4. Ablations over lambda Neumann depth K and CG iters T. Verify that nonzero lambda improves conditioning and that K equals 5 or T equals 10 suffices within the operating regime.\\n5. Failure-mode log for divergence events. Include conditioning diagnostics from the last solve.\\n\\nControls. Three seeds per arm. Same inner-step count across arms within each task tier. Shared inner-network initialization distribution deterministic given seed. Separate PRNG streams for data generation and task sampling so the held-out meta-test tasks are unseen. Sanity probe: with alpha equals 0 every implicit arm must return the FOMAML gradient.\\n\\nFeasibility check. Toy regression and sine-wave run on CPU in seconds to minutes per arm per seed. Synthetic N-way K-shot ConvNet at meta-batch 4 tasks times 25 examples is the heavy arm; estimated minutes per iteration on the 24-core box. 20000 iterations times 5 arms times 3 seeds runs multi-hour but within an overnight CPU budget. The design cannot be executed in this bubblewrap because torch is absent; the design size is nevertheless CPU-realistic. Memory expectation is that the implicit arm holds O(params) extra activations versus EXPLICIT-MAML's O(params times K_inner) and this expectation is treated as a result to be measured.\\n\\n## 3. Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\nBlocks-approval items (must be resolved before I draft the final design).\\n- Which TorchOpt version and which implicit-differentiation entry point is intended. Among torchopt.MetaGradientModule torchopt.diff.implicit_diff-style wrapper torchopt.MetaSGD plus manual CG or Neumann or an Optax-style optax.implicit_diff. blocks approval.\\n- What is the target of differentiation. (a) the inner-loop initialization theta-zero only classical iMAML or (b) per-parameter inner learning rates and initialization MetaSGD-style or (c) outer hyperparameters such as regularization weight. blocks approval.\\n- Second-order implicit versus first-order implicit. Must the design include full Gauss-Newton solve or is