{"id": "44e4ce95-b02d-4558-9c15-5bdde559c51f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:48:46.511694", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0ee9d52c-4232-4065-b0a2-70cd42f67406", "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /workspace/notes && cat > /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART1'\n# Final Experiment Design -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\n\n## 0. Status and portability statement\n\nThis document is a design. No experiment has been run. No numerical results are reported. The design is written so a third party can pick it up on a CPU-only Linux machine with 24 logical cores, install TorchOpt and PyTorch on the user's own Python environment, and execute it without further clarification.\n\nThe design is portable across TorchOpt versions. Where a TorchOpt symbol is named below it is based on the canonical v0.9-style differentiable-meta interface (MetaGradientModule / functional inner solver with an implicit-diff wrapper). Anything not locally verifiable from the present bubblewrap (which has no TorchOpt installed and no network) is explicitly tagged with the prefix `assume v0.9-style:` so the implementing engineer knows to validate against the actually-installed TorchOpt version. The fallback implementation makes the design environment-agnostic.\n\n## 1. Research question and rationale\n\nOperational question. Does implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) produce meta-gradients that match the analytic or second-order MAML gradient to within tolerance epsilon, (b) train a meta-learner to held-out accuracy or loss comparable to or better than explicit unrolled MAML given a fixed inner-step budget, and (c) do so with lower per-meta-iteration wall-clock time and lower inner-trajectory memory than unrolled second-order MAML?\n\nAlgorithmic rationale. Let phi denote the meta-parameters (an initialization of the inner network plus for the MetaSGD arm per-parameter inner learning rates), theta_t the inner parameters at step t, alpha the inner learning rate, and L_inner(theta; D_support) the support-task loss. Inner SGD gives theta_{t+1} = theta_t - alpha * grad_theta L_inner(theta_t; D). At convergence theta_t approximately theta_star(phi) is determined by the implicit equation 0 = g(theta_star, phi) := theta_star - phi + alpha * grad_theta L_inner(theta_star; D) (using the per-parameter MetaSGD model when applicable). Implicit-function theorem gives dtheta_star/dphi = (I - alpha * H_inner)^{-1} where H_inner is the Hessian of alpha times L_inner with respect to theta at the fixed point. The outer meta-gradient is grad_phi L_query(theta_star(phi); D_query) = (dtheta_star/dphi)^T * grad_theta L_query(theta_star; D_query). In practice the inverse is not formed.\n\nThree solve strategies are compared. Truncated Neumann series: dtheta_star/dphi * v approximately Sum_{k=0}^{K-1} (alpha * H_inner)^k * v. Conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system (H_inner + lambda*I) * z = grad_theta L_query with T outer iterations. Exact solve (oracle reference) on the tiny toy only.\n\nThe rationale for expecting implicit-diff to win on (b) and (c) is well established (iMAML beats first-order MAML on Omniglot and MiniImageNet while avoiding the O(params * K_inner) memory cost of unrolling) and is what the experiment is designed to test under the specifically-TorchOpt-mediated implementation path.\n\nHypothesis summary. H1 (gradient fidelity): on the toy linear task with K_inner equals 1, the second-order implicit meta-gradient matches the closed-form analytic meta-gradient to within relative L2 error at most 1e-2 for the exact solver; the Neumann and CG arms approach this as K or T grow. H2 (meta-test non-inferiority): on the sine and classification tasks, each implicit arm has a 95 percent confidence interval on the meta-test metric that overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML while improving per-iteration wall-clock OR peak memory (Pareto domination on at least one axis). H3 (memory decoupling): peak resident memory for the implicit arms is approximately O(params) and independent of K_inner within the sweep, while EXPLICIT-MAML scales as approximately O(params * K_inner).\n\nBaselines. EXPLICIT-MAML: unroll K_inner inner steps with retain_graph equal True on PyTorch or functional torchopt.MetaGradientModule. Reference for gradient fidelity and accuracy upper bound given unrolling. FOMAML: same unrolling but detach inner gradients at the support/query boundary. First-order reference and lower-cost baseline. REPTILE (Nichol et al. 2018): non-unrolled first-order baseline that interpolates meta-parameters toward task-adapted parameters after K_inner unrolled inner steps; serves as a control that performs no second-order correction at all. The FOMAML/REPTILE pair and the EXPLICIT-MAML pair jointly separate second-order information content (which only EXPLICIT-MAML has) from memory cost of unrolling (which only EXPLICIT-MAML pays).\n\nPART1\necho \"part1 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-527f05a2-87aa-4fd4-99a1-e341afd2f560", "tool_call": {"id": "call-527f05a2-87aa-4fd4-99a1-e341afd2f560", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Write part 1 of final_design.md\", \"command\": \"mkdir -p /workspace/notes && cat > /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART1'\\n# Final Experiment Design -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\n## 0. Status and portability statement\\n\\nThis document is a design. No experiment has been run. No numerical results are reported. The design is written so a third party can pick it up on a CPU-only Linux machine with 24 logical cores, install TorchOpt and PyTorch on the user's own Python environment, and execute it without further clarification.\\n\\nThe design is portable across TorchOpt versions. Where a TorchOpt symbol is named below it is based on the canonical v0.9-styl