pproximately Sum_{k=0}^{K-1} (alpha * H_inner)^k * v. Conjugate gradient on the damped Gauss-Newton system (H_inner + lambda*I) * z = grad_theta L_query with T outer iterations. Exact solve (oracle reference) on the tiny toy only.\\n\\nThe rationale for expecting implicit-diff to win on (b) and (c) is well established (iMAML beats first-order MAML on Omniglot and MiniImageNet while avoiding the O(params * K_inner) memory cost of unrolling) and is what the experiment is designed to test under the specifically-TorchOpt-mediated implementation path.\\n\\nHypothesis summary. H1 (gradient fidelity): on the toy linear task with K_inner equals 1, the second-order implicit meta-gradient matches the closed-form analytic meta-gradient to within relative L2 error at most 1e-2 for the exact solver; the Neumann and CG arms approach this as K or T grow. H2 (meta-test non-inferiority): on the sine and classification tasks, each implicit arm has a 95 percent confidence interval on the meta-test metric that overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML while improving per-iteration wall-clock OR peak memory (Pareto domination on at least one axis). H3 (memory decoupling): peak resident memory for the implicit arms is approximately O(params) and independent of K_inner within the sweep, while EXPLICIT-MAML scales as approximately O(params * K_inner).\\n\\nBaselines. EXPLICIT-MAML: unroll K_inner inner steps with retain_graph equal True on PyTorch or functional torchopt.MetaGradientModule. Reference for gradient fidelity and accuracy upper bound given unrolling. FOMAML: same unrolling but detach inner gradients at the support/query boundary. First-order reference and lower-cost baseline. REPTILE (Nichol et al. 2018): non-unrolled first-order baseline that interpolates meta-parameters toward task-adapted parameters after K_inner unrolled inner steps; serves as a control that performs no second-order correction at all. The FOMAML/REPTILE pair and the EXPLICIT-MAML pair jointly separate second-order information