{"id": "975ee482-6cd8-4b04-a093-31a26639a82c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:48:59.716949", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "421a1231-9706-4030-a8a8-47ec8dde0697", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART2'\n\n## 2. Tiered implementation architecture\n\nThe design is shipped behind a single meta_solver.py module exposing the interface\n\n    solver.solve_meta_grad(\n        inner_net,\n        inner_opt_spec,\n        support_batch,\n        query_batch,\n        meta_params,\n        inner_steps,         # K_inner\n        inner_lr,            # alpha\n        method,              # one of neumann, cg, exact, explicit, fomaml, reptile\n        neumann_depth=None,  # K\n        cg_iters=None,       # T\n        damping=None,        # lambda\n    ) -> (meta_grad, aux_dict)\n\naux_dict carries per-iteration diagnostics: CG residuals, Neumann tail norm, peak bytes held, wall-clock seconds, divergence flag.\n\nTwo interchangeable bodies fill this interface.\n\nTier A -- TorchOpt-native (primary path). Inner-optimizer construction via `torchopt.MetaSGD(meta_net, lr=alpha)` for the secondary MetaSGD arm, plain `torchopt.SGD(alpha)` for the primary arm. `assume v0.9-style:` named entries used: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule`, `torchopt.functional`, `torchopt.diff.implicit_diff` (or `torchopt.implicit_diff`), and the functional inner loop obtained via `torchopt.MetaGradientModule(meta_net, inner_opt)` s `meta_opt.step(loss)` / `meta_opt.compute_gradients(loss)` API. Validate against the installed version.\n\nHessian-vector products via the PyTorch double-backward primitive `torch.autograd.grad` applied twice, or via functorch jvp and vjp over `torch.func.functional_call`, which is what TorchOpt s differentiable meta module itself composes. `assume v0.9-style:` the helper `torchopt.diff.hvp` may exist and is used if present. Neumann / CG / exact assembled using the functional inner parameters returned by TorchOpt s functional meta utilities.\n\nTier B -- TorchOpt-free (fallback). All operations through `torch.func.functional_call`, `torch.func.grad`, `torch.func.vmap`, and double-backward `torch.autograd.grad`. The implicit-grad identity dtheta_star/dphi equals (I minus alpha H) inverse times alpha times mixed-derivative is evaluated either by truncated Neumann or by `torch.linalg.solve` (toy only) or by an in-script CG loop over a HVP oracle.\n\nThe two tiers are mechanically identical and identical up to floating-point determinism modulo the choice of HVP backend. A pytest-style test in tests/test_solver_equivalence.py runs both tiers on the toy task with K_inner equals 1 and asserts the gradient-relative-L2 error between them is at most 1e-6; this catches API drift in TorchOpt versions as soon as the engineer installs one.\n\n## 3. Tasks (default: synthetic only -- no online data)\n\nThree tasks that together separate the gradient-fidelity claim from the meta-learning claim.\n\n### 3.1 Toy linear regression (analytic oracle)\nData: y equals w_star dot x plus epsilon with x in R^d, d=10, w_star sampled from N(0 I), epsilon sampled from N(0 sigma^2), sigma=0.1. Inner step updates a linear regressor with one gradient step on the squared error. K_inner=1. Support: K_shot=10 examples per task. Query: K_query=20 examples per task. Why: with K_inner=1 the meta-gradient has a closed form (the K_inner=1 linear-MAML case). This is the only task where the closed-form analytic meta-gradient is computable; therefore it is the only task where H1 s at-most-1e-2 threshold is meaningful.\n\n### 3.2 Sine-wave few-shot regression\nData: y equals a times sin(x minus b), amplitude a sampled from U(0.1, 5.0), phase b sampled from U(0, pi), x sampled from U(-5, 5), epsilon sampled from N(0, 0.1^2). Standard Finn et al. 2017 protocol. Support K_shot=10, query K_query=10. 4 inner gradient-update steps for adaptation at test time. Inner network: MLP 2x40 ReLU. Inner loss is mean squared error.\n\n### 3.3 Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot classification\nData: N=5 classes drawn from Gaussian mixtures in R^d=400; per-class mean drawn uniformly within a margin band, shared covariance. Inter-class margin is a fixed hyperparameter (default: mean separation 4 times marginal standard deviation) to keep the task measurable but not trivial. Support K_shot=5 per class. Query K_query=15 per class. Inner loss is cross-entropy. Inner network: 4-layer ConvNet (64ch, 3x3, BN, ReLU, max-pool) -- exactly the MAML conv net used in MiniImageNet literature -- adapted to 5-way classification on 28x28 grayscale inputs. Synthetic so the experiment needs no downloads.\n\nPer task: separate PRNG streams for data generation and for task sampling (see section 6 controls). 600 held-out test tasks per arm (3 seeds times 200).\nPART2\necho \"part2 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-ba2587a9-ed55-412e-9aa2-e5216d63eeb7", "tool_call": {"id": "call-ba2587a9-ed55-412e-9aa2-e5216d63eeb7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append part 2 of final_design.md\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART2'\\n\\n## 2. Tiered implementation architecture\\n\\nThe design is shipped behind a single meta_solver.py module exposing the interface\\n\\n    solver.solve_meta_grad(\\n        inner_net,\\n        inner_opt_spec,\\n        support_batch,\\n        query_batch,\\n        meta_params,\\n        inner_steps,         # K_inner\\n        inner_lr,            # alpha\\n        method,              # one of neumann, cg, exact, explicit, fomaml, reptile\\n        neumann_depth=None,  # K\\n        cg_iters=None,       # T\\n        damping=None,        # lambda\\n    ) -> (meta_grad, aux_dict)\\n\\naux_dict carries per-iteration diagnostics: CG residuals, Neumann tail norm, peak bytes held, wall-clock seconds, divergence flag.\\n\\nTwo 