omposes. `assume v0.9-style:` the helper `torchopt.diff.hvp` may exist and is used if present. Neumann / CG / exact assembled using the functional inner parameters returned by TorchOpt s functional meta utilities.\\n\\nTier B -- TorchOpt-free (fallback). All operations through `torch.func.functional_call`, `torch.func.grad`, `torch.func.vmap`, and double-backward `torch.autograd.grad`. The implicit-grad identity dtheta_star/dphi equals (I minus alpha H) inverse times alpha times mixed-derivative is evaluated either by truncated Neumann or by `torch.linalg.solve` (toy only) or by an in-script CG loop over a HVP oracle.\\n\\nThe two tiers are mechanically identical and identical up to floating-point determinism modulo the choice of HVP backend. A pytest-style test in tests/test_solver_equivalence.py runs both tiers on the toy task with K_inner equals 1 and asserts the gradient-relative-L2 error between them is at most 1e-6; this catches API drift in TorchOpt versions as soon as the engineer installs one.\\n\\n## 3. Tasks (default: synthetic only -- no online data)\\n\\nThree tasks that together separate the gradient-fidelity claim from the meta-learning claim.\\n\\n### 3.1 Toy linear regression (analytic oracle)\\nData: y equals w_star dot x plus epsilon with x in R^d, d=10, w_star sampled from N(0 I), epsilon sampled from N(0 sigma^2), sigma=0.1. Inner step updates a linear regressor with one gradient step on the squared error. K_inner=1. Support: K_shot=10 examples per task. Query: K_query=20 examples per task. Why: with K_inner=1 the meta-gradient has a closed form (the K_inner=1 linear-MAML case). This is the only task where the closed-form analytic meta-gradient is computable; therefore it is the only task where H1 s at-most-1e-2 threshold is meaningful.\\n\\n### 3.2 Sine-wave few-shot regression\\nData: y equals a times sin(x minus b), amplitude a sampled from U(0.1, 5.0), phase b sampled from U(0, pi), x sampled from U(-5, 5), epsilon sampled from N(0, 0.1^2). Standard Finn et al. 2017 protocol. Support K_shot=10, query K_query=10. 4 inner gradient-update steps for adaptation at test time. Inner network: MLP 2x40 ReLU. Inner loss is mean squared error.\\n\\n### 3.3 Synthetic Gaussian-mixture N-way K-shot classification\\nData: N=5 classes drawn from Gaussian mixtures in R^d=400; per-class mean drawn uniformly within a margin band, shared covariance. Inter-class margin is a fixed hyperparameter (default: mean separation 4 times marginal standard deviation) to keep the task measurable but not trivial. Support K_shot=5 per class. Query K_query=15 per class. Inner loss is cross-entropy. Inner network: 4-layer ConvNet (64ch, 3x3, BN, ReLU, max-pool) -- exactly the MAML conv net used in MiniImageNet literature -- adapted to 5-way classification on 28x28 grayscale inputs. Synthetic so the experiment needs no downloads.\\n\\nPer task: separate PRNG streams for data generation and for task sampling (see section 6 controls). 600 held-out test tasks per arm (3 seeds times 200).\\nPART2\\necho \\\"part2 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\\\"\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.008, \"output\": \"part2 done: 67 lines\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "975ee482-6cd8-4b04-a093-31a26639a82c", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 47}
{"id": "a8abf865-0dd8-4a69-b475-64bb0a4803e9", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:49:17.079774", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d64adff5-ab9e-486a-a484-78dbeabc85c5", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART3'\n\n## 4. Networks and optimizers\n\nRegression arms: MLP with input to 40 to 40 to 1, ReLU activations, linear output. About 1700 parameters. Hosted entirely on CPU. Classification arm: 4-layer ConvNet (conv 3 to 64 3x3, BN, ReLU, maxpool 2, conv 64 to 64 3x3, BN, ReLU, maxpool 2, flatten, linear 1600 to 800, BN, ReLU, linear 800 to 5). About 110000 parameters. Hosted entirely on CPU at meta-batch at most 4 tasks times 25 examples. Inner optimizer: SGD with per-parameter inner LR via `torchopt.MetaSGD(meta_net, lr=alpha)` in the MetaSGD secondary arm; plain SGD in the primary arm. Inner LR alpha drawn from {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}. Meta-optimizer: `torch.optim.Adam` at outer LR beta equals 1e-3. The meta-learner updates only the meta-parameters theta_0 and (MetaSGD arm only) the per-parameter inner LRs.\n\n## 5. Outer loop and implicit-solver hyperparameters\n\n| Parameter | Default | Sweep | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Inner LR alpha | 0.04 | {0.01, 0.04, 0.1} | Sanity probe: alpha=0 must reproduce FOMAML |\n| Outer LR beta | 1e-3 | fixed | Adam |\n| Inner step count K_inner | 5 | {1, 5, 10} | Classification default 5 |\n| Damping lambda | 1e-2 | {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1} | Applied as (H + lambda*I); nonzero lambda required for convergence of CG |\n| Neumann depth K | 5 | {3, 5, 10} | Truncation order |\n| CG iters T | 10 | {5, 10, 20} | Outer loop iteration budget |\n| CG tolerance | 1e-5 | fixed | Early-stop inside CG |\n| Meta batch (tasks/step) | reg 4, cls 4 | reg {2, 8}, cls {2, 4} | Tasks per outer step |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} | fixed | Three replications |\n| Toy meta iters | 5000 | fixed | Per arm per seed |\n| Sine meta iters | 10000 | fixed | Per arm per seed |\n| Classification meta iters | 20000 | fixed | Per arm per seed |\n\nTotal arms: 5 methods times 3 inner-LR values times 3 K_inner times 3 lambda values times (where applicable K or T sweep) times 3 seeds. The IMPLICIT-EXACT and analytic-oracle arms run only on the toy task. The lambda ablation runs only on the implicit arms. Pseudo-factorized sweep cap is set per-task to stay under the section 8 feasibility ceiling.\n\n## 6. Controls\n\n| Control name | What it controls | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fixed seed set | seeds {0, 1, 2} per arm | Confidence interval on meta-test metrics |\n| Separate PRNG streams | one for data generation, one for task sampling, one for inner initialization | Test tasks are unseen; reproducibility |\n| Deterministic inner-init sampling | seeded RNG, same draws across arms given task index | Isolates inner-init randomness from solver randomneess |\n| Sanity probe (alpha=0) | Run implicit arms with alpha=0 and verify gradient matches FOMAML | Catches bugs in the implicit-diff implementation that silently degenerate to wrong code |\n| Method parity table | All arms share the same inner loss, inner optimizer class, inner init distribution, data generation, and meta-batch size | Isolates the meta-gradient computation from the surrounding machinery |\n| NaN / divergence detector | Per outer step flagged if any parameter goes NaN/Inf or CG residual fails to drop by 5 times within T iterations | Stability metric, included in auxiliary logs |\n| Solver equivalence test | Tier A vs Tier B on toy K_inner=1 must have relative-L2 error at most 1e-6 | Catches TorchOpt API drift across versions |\n\nREPTILE control specifically excludes any second-order correction or implicit solve, providing a non-unrolled comparison cliff.\n\n## 7. Metrics\n\n1. Gradient-fidelity metric (toy regression only). Per outer step, store the meta-gradient returned by each arm as well as the closed-form analytic meta-gradient (the K_inner=1 case has it). Compute the relative L2 error and report mean and std across seeds. H1 requires this to be at most 1e-2 for IMPLICIT-EXACT and to monotonically decrease in K (Neumann) and T (CG) for IMPLICIT-NEUMANN and IMPLICIT-CG. The same metric against the unrolled-K second-order gradient acts as a proxy on tasks where K_inner > 1.\n\n2. Inner-loss trajectory (all tasks). For a fixed test-task batch, plot L_query versus inner adaptation step after adapting on the support. Mean and std across 600 test tasks and 3 seeds. The implicit arm s first adaptation step parameter must yield a value within numerical tolerance of EXPLICIT-MAML s first adaptation step parameter when run at the same K_inner and alpha -- this is a sanity equivalence rather than a performance metric.\n\n3. Meta-test performance. Sine arm: mean-squared error on the 600-task held-out split. Classification arm: 5-way accuracy (in percentage points). 95 percent confidence intervals computed from the 3 seed replications using the student-t distribution over seed-mean estimates.\n\n4. Per-iteration wall-clock. Time per outer meta-iteration broken into (a) inner adaptation loop and (b) outer-step solve, measured with time.perf_counter. Reported as mean plus/minus std over 100 iterations after a 50-iteration warm-up.\n\n5. Peak memory. resource.getrusage deltas, plus an instrumented accounting of bytes held by the autograd retain_graph store (param count times K_inner times dtype size for EXPLICIT-MAML; param count times (1 + Hessian bandwidth) for implicit). For H3 the implicit arm s peak memory is expected to be approximately K_inner-independent within the sweep while EXPLICIT-MAML scales linearly.\n\n6. Stability. Fraction of iterations flagged by the divergence detector under each lambda and K (or T). Optional: per-event log of (alpha, lambda, K, K_inner, last residual).\nPART3\necho \"part3 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-83ce9ce5-8177-4691-af64-d521aa3300f9", "tool_call": {"i