{"id": "15c56c4b-20f6-4460-b85a-c813bde3f1c0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:49:33.203609", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "da5279e2-702e-4aab-92de-832ffb14fc03", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART4'\n\n## 8. Feasibility assessment\n\nCompute ceiling: the design must run on a 24-CPU Linux / no-GPU machine overnight (at most 12 hours wall-clock per arm-set per seed). Per-task iteration caps below reflect this.\n\n| Task | Meta iters / arm / seed | Time / outer step estimate (CPU, 24 cores) | Wall-clock per arm per seed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Toy linear regression | 5000 | at most about 10 ms (tiny linear net, K_inner=1) | at most about 50 s |\n| Sine-wave regression | 10000 | at most about 100 ms (MLP-40, K_inner=5, meta batch 4) | at most about 17 min |\n| Gaussian-mixture classification | 20000 | at most about 500 ms (ConvNet-110K, K_inner=5, meta batch 4) | at most about 2.8 hr |\n| REPTILE baselines | mirror the same caps | generally faster than EXPLICIT-MAML; same wall-clock by construction | within the same envelopes |\n| Factorized sweep cap | For sine and classification the IMPLICIT-NEUMANN x lambda x K and IMPLICIT-CG x lambda x T grids are run at a single alpha (the central 0.04) and a single K_inner per task (5 for sine, 5 for classification) to stay within feasibility. Out-of-default alpha and K_inner are run only for the toy task and only for the implicit arms. |\n\nTotal budget at the full sweep, summed across arms, seeds, and task tiers: at most 8 hours wall-clock per (method x task) envelope times 3 seeds -- within the overnight ceiling. Worst case (all 5 arms times 3 seeds times both task tiers) at most about 12 hours on the 24-CPU box. The IMPLICIT-EXACT arm runs only on toy and is negligible. Verification step: run a single (method=IMPLICIT-CG, task=sine, seed=0) end-to-end during the engineer s setup, and confirm that 1000 iterations fits in at most 2 minutes before launching the full sweep -- if not, drop meta-batch to 2 first.\n\nDisk footprint: negligible. Checkpoints stored only at end-of-run per (arm x task x seed). No online data; no caching of test tasks to disk beyond about 600 task metadata blobs.\n\nEnvironment assumptions: PyTorch at least 2.0 with functorch; TorchOpt installed per the user s chosen version (the Tier-B fallback substitutes if TorchOpt is not available). System Python or any working venv with these installed. If TorchOpt is unavailable in the target env, Tier B fully substitutes; no other changes are needed.\n\n## 9. Analysis plan\n\nDecision rule -- what makes the experiment a win: Pareto-front dominance on the (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory) triple, with the additional constraint that the implicit arm s 95 percent CI on the meta-test metric overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML s 95 percent CI (per the success criterion).\n\n1. Gradient-fidelity table. Rows = arm (EXPLICIT-MAML, IMPLICIT-NEUMANN K in {3, 5, 10}, IMPLICIT-CG T in {5, 10, 20}, IMPLICIT-EXACT). Columns = K_inner in {1, 5, 10}. Cell = mean relative-L2 error in the meta-gradient vs the analytic oracle, std across seeds. Reported for the toy task; a proxy table on sine replaces analytic oracle with unrolled-K second-order gradient.\n\n2. Meta-test learning curves. x = meta iteration. y = held-out MSE (sine) or 5-way accuracy (classification). One curve per arm with shaded 95 percent CI band from the 3 seeds. Required to demonstrate convergence behavior under matching compute, not just final value.\n\n3. Wall-clock-corrected curves. Same y, x = cumulative wall-clock seconds. Exposes the speed and accuracy tradeoff directly.\n\n4. Pareto front. For each task, scatter (per-iteration wall-clock, peak memory) colored by arm, with peak meta-test as the marker size. Overlay the EXPLICIT-MAML frontier and explicit dominance regions. An implicit arm is a Pareto win if it is non-dominated and non-inferior on the meta-test metric (CI overlap).\n\n5. Ablations. Lambda-curve: peak meta-test vs lambda for IMPLICIT-NEUMANN (K=5 fixed) and IMPLICIT-CG (T=10 fixed). Expect a U-shape with the minimum in the {1e-3, 1e-2} band. Neumann depth K: peak meta-test vs K for IMPLICIT-NEUMANN at lambda=1e-2. Expect monotone improvement with diminishing returns. CG iters T: peak meta-test vs T for IMPLICIT-CG at lambda=1e-2. Expect monotone improvement until residual noise floor. Alpha x K_inner sweep on toy only.\n\n6. Stability diagnostics. Histograms of CG residuals at the final outer step under each (lambda, T) setting; fraction of divergence events versus alpha.\n\n7. Memory-scaling plot. Peak resident memory versus K_inner for EXPLICIT-MAML and the implicit arms, on the sine task. H3 expected to manifest as a flat implicit arm curve and a slope-approximately-1 line for EXPLICIT-MAML.\n\n8. Solver equivalence check. Tier-A vs Tier-B gradient-relative-error report on the toy K_inner=1 case; if at most 1e-6, the rest of the results are reported trusting Tier A (the implementer s actual choice) but with the assurance that Tier B is a faithful fallback.\n\nDecision rule restated. An implicit arm wins iff its 95 percent CI on meta-test overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML AND its wall-clock per iter OR peak memory is strictly better than EXPLICIT-MAML. Multi-axis Pareto wins (improvement on both) are highlighted separately.\nPART4\necho \"part4 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-91137b86-33d4-4999-90c0-dc48c52f8588", "tool_call": {"id": "call-91137b86-33d4-4999-90c0-dc48c52f8588", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append part 4 of final_design.md\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART4'\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility assessment\\n\\nCompute ceiling: the design must run on a 24-CPU Linux / no-GPU machine overnight (at most 12 hours wall-clock per arm-set per seed). Per-task iteration caps 