{"id": "2691e8a4-e512-46ea-8edd-28bdcbc0b728", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:49:48.495814", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "36e13a7a-dc58-4246-87a9-9e9e71a137c8", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART5'\n\n## 10. Risks and known gotchas\n\n- retain_graph equal True for EXPLICIT-MAML. The implicit arms do not retain the inner autograd graph; EXPLICIT-MAML does. Memory differential is therefore measured directly and reported as such (see H3).\n- Neumann convergence condition. The Neumann series converges only if alpha times the Hessian operator norm is strictly below 1. This is checked empirically during the sweep; if violated at any alpha, that alpha is dropped from the implicit-vs-explicit comparison on that task and flagged in the analysis.\n- Hessian-vector product stability. Double-backward HVP can be numerically unstable at small alpha unless the inner loss is rescaled. The fallback path uses torch.func.jvp and vjp to compute the Hessian-vector product directly, which avoids second-order backward and is therefore preferred for HVP accuracy. The Tier-A path uses the helper if available (assume v0.9-style: torchopt.diff.hvp) and otherwise falls back through torch.autograd.grad twice.\n- TorchOpt API drift. Tier-A code names are validated against the installed TorchOpt version. The Tier-B fallback guarantees the experiment can still run if TorchOpt is unavailable or has different naming. The section 2 solver-equivalence test catches drift on the toy task.\n- REPTILE needs special handling. REPTILE does not compute a per-step meta-gradient but interpolates from inner-adapted task parameters back to the meta-parameters after K_inner steps. Implemented as a separate arm, not as a {method: reptile} plug into the same solver, so the metrics are computed identically for fair comparison.\n- Synthetic Gaussian mixture is not a verified stand-in for MiniImageNet. The classification arm is designed to be informative about feasibility and the algorithmic differential, not to substitute a benchmark-claim about MiniImageNet accuracy. The deliverable makes this explicit in section 3.3 and section 11.\n\n## 11. Repository / file layout for portable execution\n\nSingle top-level directory layout, no external dependencies beyond PyTorch + (optionally) TorchOpt:\n\n    /workspace/\n      task.json\n      notes/\n        initial_inspection.md\n        final_design.md      # this document\n        design_summary.md    # submission deliverable\n      meta_solver.py         # single interface, Tier A and Tier B\n      tasks/\n        toy_linear.py\n        sine_wave.py\n        gaussian_mixture.py\n      nets/\n        mlp40.py\n        convnet4.py\n      baselines/\n        explicit_maml.py\n        fomaml.py\n        reptile.py\n      arms/\n        neumann.py\n        cg.py\n        exact.py              # toy only\n      analysis/\n        gradient_fidelity.py\n        learning_curves.py\n        pareto.py\n        memory_scaling.py\n        ablation_lambda.py\n        ablation_K_T.py\n        stability.py\n      run_sweep.py\n      tests/\n        test_solver_equivalence.py   # Tier A vs Tier B\n        test_alpha_zero_sanity.py    # alpha=0 reproduces FOMAML\n        test_seed_separation.py      # PRNG streams are independent\n\nEach analysis script consumes experiment artifacts emitted by run_sweep.py and emits PNGs plus a JSON metrics summary.\n\n## 12. Reproduction recipe\n\n1. Install Python at least 3.10 with PyTorch at least 2.0 and (optionally) TorchOpt. If TorchOpt is unavailable, the Tier-B code path runs without modification.\n2. `python tests/test_solver_equivalence.py` -- must pass; otherwise investigate.\n3. `python run_sweep.py --task toy --methods all --seeds 0 1 2` -- sanity run, completes in minutes.\n4. `python run_sweep.py --task sine --methods all --seeds 0 1 2` -- at most 2 hours.\n5. `python run_sweep.py --task classification --methods all --seeds 0 1 2` -- at most 9 hours.\n6. `python analysis/gradient_fidelity.py` -- produces table.\n7. `python analysis/learning_curves.py` and `wall_clock_curves.py` -- produce plots.\n8. `python analysis/pareto.py` -- applies the decision rule.\n9. Ablations: `analysis/ablation_lambda.py`, `analysis/ablation_K_T.py`, `analysis/memory_scaling.py`, `analysis/stability.py`.\n10. Report following the metric list in section 7 and the decision rule in section 9.\n\nIf the sanity run produces a gradient-relative-L2 greater than 1e-2 on the toy task or the alpha=0 sanity probe fails to return FOMAML, STOP and debug before launching the full sweep.\n\n## 13. What this design does not claim\n\n- No online benchmarks, no MiniImageNet or CIFAR-FS or Omniglot numbers.\n- No fabricated empirical numbers of any kind. All quantitative statements are asymptotic or follow from algorithmic identity (for example O(params) vs O(params * K_inner) memory differential of the implicit-vs-explicit arms).\n- No claim about TorchOpt behavior beyond what is publicly known about its differentiable meta-learning module. Any specific TorchOpt symbol named in this document carries the `assume v0.9-style:` tag and is to be validated against the installed version by the engineer.\nPART5\necho \"part5 done: $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/final_design.md) lines\"\necho \"---\"\nhead -5 /workspace/notes/final_design.md\necho \"...\"\ntail -5 /workspace/notes/final_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-924d99d5-c274-4383-aa1e-8674a465919e", "tool_call": {"id": "call-924d99d5-c274-4383-aa1e-8674a465919e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append part 5 closing sections\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/notes/final_design.md <<'PART5'\\n\\n## 10. Risks and known gotchas\\n\\n- retain_graph equal True for EXPLICIT-MAML. The implicit arms do not retain the inner autograd graph; EXPLICIT-MAML does. Memory differential is therefore me