{"id": "30333e51-4a3a-4682-9cdc-bd49fec5f5f3", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:50:12.768031", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9c41e66d-cf72-4206-a320-167fff06e950", "action": {"command": "cat > /workspace/notes/design_summary.md <<'SUMMARY'\n# Design Summary -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\n\nThis document is the submission deliverable for the experiment design task. Full details are at /workspace/notes/final_design.md. Inspection notes are at /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md.\n\nNo experiment has been run. No numerical results are reported. The submission is a portable design spec, written so a third party can execute it on a CPU-only 24-core machine.\n\n## Operational question\n\nDoes implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) reproduce the analytic or second-order MAML meta-gradient on a toy regression arm to within relative L2 error at most 1e-2, (b) train a meta-learner to a held-out meta-test 95 percent CI that overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML while strictly improving per-iteration wall-clock OR peak memory on sine-wave regression and on a synthetic 5-way 5-shot classification arm, and (c) decouple peak memory from the inner step count K_inner (expected O(params) versus O(params times K_inner))?\n\n## Algorithmic core\n\nTreat the inner-loop fixed point as a function of the meta-parameters phi and apply the implicit-function theorem to obtain\n\n    dtheta_star/dphi = (I minus alpha times H_inner)^{-1}\n\nThree solve strategies are compared: truncated Neumann (depth K), conjugate gradient on (H + lambda I) with iterations T, and exact solve (toy only). H_inner is evaluated without ever materializing the full Hessian, using either double-backward torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp and vjp. Outer meta gradient is the chain rule through this solved inverse with the query loss Jacobian.\n\n## Method arms\n\nEXPLICIT-MAML, FOMAML, REPTILE, IMPLICIT-NEUMANN K, IMPLICIT-CG T, IMPLICIT-EXACT (toy only). Six arms total. EXPLICIT-MAML is the accuracy upper bound. FOMAML and REPTILE together separate first-order information content from second-order. The three implicit arms are the experimental subject. REPTILE is the non-unrolled first-order control that performs no second-order correction.\n\n## TorchOpt interface (portable spec)\n\nPrimary path (Tier A, `assume v0.9-style:` symbols to validate): torchopt.MetaGradientModule for the differentiable inner loop with a functional inner optimizer, the implicit-diff wrapper at torchopt.diff.implicit_diff (or torchopt.implicit_diff) applied over the inner fixed point, and the inner optimizer constructed via torchopt.MetaSGD for the secondary MetaSGD arm or plain torchopt.SGD otherwise. Hessian-vector products double through torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp/vjp.\n\nFallback path (Tier B): the same algorithmic identities implemented via torch.func.functional_call plus torch.func.grad plus torch.func.vmap and an in-script CG loop. The two paths are connected by a single meta_solver.py module with a uniform interface so swapping Tier A for Tier B is a one-file change. A solver-equivalence test on the toy task asserts gradient relative L2 at most 1e-6 between tiers to catch TorchOpt API drift.\n\n## Tasks\n\nSynthetic only (network disabled, no online mirror). Three tasks.\n\n- Toy linear regression with d=10, sigma=0.1, K_inner=1, K_shot=10, K_query=20. Unique in that the meta-gradient has a closed form; the gradient-fidelity threshold of relative L2 at most 1e-2 is defined here against the analytic oracle.\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 protocol), amplitude in [0.1, 5.0], phase in [0, pi], K_shot=10, K_query=10, 4 inner updates. MLP 2x40 ReLU.\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture 5-way 5-shot classification with controllable inter-class margin, K_query=15 per class, K_inner=5, 4-layer ConvNet (MAML MiniImageNet style), 28x28 grayscale inputs.\n\n## Networks and optimizers\n\nMLP 2x40 ReLU (about 1700 params) for regression; 4-layer ConvNet with BN and max-pool (about 110000 params) for classification. Inner SGD with per-parameter inner LR (torchopt.MetaSGD primary; torch.optim.SGD fallback). Outer Adam at outer LR 1e-3. Inner LR alpha in {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}. K_inner in {1, 5, 10}. Damping lambda in {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}, default 1e-2. Neumann depth K in {3, 5, 10}, default 5. CG iters T in {5, 10, 20}, default 10, tolerance 1e-5.\n\n## Controls\n\nThree seeds {0, 1, 2}. Separate PRNG streams for data generation, task sampling, and inner initialization. Deterministic inner init given seed. Sanity probe with alpha=0 must reproduce FOMAML. Method parity table across all arms. NaN/divergence detector. Tier A versus Tier B solver-equivalence test. REPTILE control.\n\n## Metrics\n\nGradient fidelity (relative L2 against analytic oracle on toy, against unrolled-K second-order on sine). Inner-loss trajectory: L_query versus inner adaptation step on the 600-task held-out split. Meta-test performance: MSE (sine), 5-way accuracy in pp (classification), both with 95 percent CIs from 3 seeds via student t. Per-iteration wall-clock split into inner and outer segments via time.perf_counter. Peak resident memory via resource.getrusage plus an instrumented accounting of retain_graph bytes. Stability: fraction of divergence events plus per-event log.\n\n## Analysis plan\n\nEight deliverables: gradient-fidelity table; meta-test learning curves; wall-clock-corrected curves; Pareto front over (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory); lambda / K / T ablations; stability diagnostics; memory-scaling plot versus K_inner; solver equivalence check. Decision rule for success: implicit arm wins iff its 95 percent CI on meta-test overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML AND its wall-clock per iter OR peak memory is strictly better than EXPLICIT-MAML. Multi-axis Pareto wins highlighted.\n\n## Success thresholds\n\n- Toy gradient fidelity at most 1e-2 relative L2 between the implicit meta-gradient and the analytic K_inner=1 meta-gradient (IMPLICIT-EXACT), with monotone decrease in K (Neumann) and T (CG) for the truncated arms.\n- Sine-wave and synthetic-classification: 95 percent CI overlap with EXPLICIT-MAML AND strict improvement on wall-clock per iter OR peak memory. Pareto-front dominance is mandatory in the analysis.\n\n## Feasibility\n\nCPU-only, 24 cores, no GPU, overnight (at most 12 hours total). Per-task iteration caps: 5000 (toy), 10000 (sine), 20000 (classification), per arm per seed. Asymptotic per-iteration estimates: about 10 ms toy, about 100 ms sine, about 500 ms classification on a 24-core box, fitting within at most 50 s / 17 min / 2.8 hours per arm per seed. Full sweep worst-case at most about 12 hours wall-clock. Verification setup step: a single (IMPLICIT-CG, sine, seed=0) run of 1000 iterations should complete in at most 2 minutes; otherwise drop meta-batch to 2 before launching.\n\nREPTILE mirrors the same caps. The IMPLICIT-EXACT arm runs only on the toy. The factorized lambda x K / lambda x T ablation is run at a single alpha and single K_inner on the sine and classification arms to stay within feasibility. Out-of-default alpha and K_inner are explored on the toy only.\n\n## What we do not claim\n\nNo online data. No MiniImageNet or CIFAR-FS or Omniglot numbers. No fabricated empirical results. No claim about TorchOpt symbols beyond `assume v0.9-style:` tags for everything not locally verified (TorchOpt is not installed in the present bubblewrap and cannot be installed). The synthetic Gaussian mixture is explicitly *not* a benchmark stand-in for MiniImageNet; it is an algorithmic-differential probe.\n\n## File layout for portable execution\n\n/workspace/top-level with `meta_solver.py` (single Tier A/B interface), `tasks/`, `nets/`, `baselines/`, `arms/`, `analysis/`, `run_sweep.py`, and `tests/`. Details in section 11 of final_design.md.\n\n## Reproduction recipe\n\n1. Install Python 3.10+ with PyTorch 2.0+ and optionally TorchOpt.\n2. Run the solver-equivalence test.\n3. Run the sanity sweep on toy.\n4. Run the sine sweep (at most 2 hours).\n5. Run the classification sweep (at most 9 hours).\n6. Run all analysis scripts and apply the decision rule.\n\nIf the sanity sweep produces gradient-relative-L2 greater than 1e-2 or the alpha=0 sanity probe fails to return FOMAML, STOP and debug before launching the full sweep.\n\n## Status\n\nFinal design complete. Awaiting researcher acknowledgement; no execution performed.\nSUMMARY\necho \"wrote $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/design_summary.md) lines to /workspace/notes/design_summary.md\"\necho \"---\"\nls -la /workspace/notes/", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-16a2a76d-d416-4244-a858-760de4cdff2b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-16a2a76d-d416-4244-a858-760de4cdff2b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Write design_summary.md submission deliverable\", \"command\": \"cat > /workspace/notes/design_summary.md <<'SUMMARY'\\n# Design Summary -- Implicit Differentiation for Meta-Learning with TorchOpt\\n\\nThis document is the submission deliverable for the experiment design task. Full details are at /workspace/notes/final_design.md. Inspection notes are at /workspace/notes/initial_inspection.md.\\n\\nNo experiment has been run. No numerical results are reported. The submission is a portable design spec, written so a third party can execute it on a CPU-only 24-core machine.\\n\\n## Operational question\\n\\nDoes implicit differentiation of the inner-loop fixed point in TorchOpt (a) reproduce the analytic or second-order MAML meta-gradient on a toy regression arm to within relative L2 error at most 1e-2, (b) train a meta-learner to a held-out meta-test 95 percent CI that overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML while strictly improving per-iteration wall-clock OR peak memory on sine-wave regression and on a synthetic 5-way 5-shot classification arm, and (c) decouple peak memory from the inner step count K_inner (expected O(params) versus O(params times K_inner))?\\n\\n## Algorithmic core\\n\\nTreat the inner-loop fixed point as a function of the meta-parameters phi and apply the implicit-function theorem to obtain\\n\\n    dtheta_star/dphi = (I minus alpha times H_inner)^{-1}\\n\\nThree solve strategies are compared: truncated Neumann (depth K), conjugate gradient on (H + lambda I) with iterations T, and exact solve (toy only). H_inner is evaluated without ever materializing the full Hessian, using either double-backward torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp and vjp. Outer meta gradient is the chain rule through this solved inverse with the query loss Jacobian.\\n\\n## Method arms\\n\\nEXPLICIT-MAML, FOMAML, REPTILE, IMPLICIT-NEUMANN K, IMPLICIT-CG T, IMPLICIT-EXACT (toy only). Six arms total. EXPLICIT-MAML is the accuracy upper bound. FOMAML and REPTILE together separate first-order information content from second-order. The three implicit arms are the experimental subject. REPTILE is the non-unrolled first-order control that performs no second-order correction.\\n\\n## TorchOpt interface (portable spec)\\n\\nPrimary path (Tier A, `assume v0.9-style:` symbols to validate): torchopt.MetaGradientModule for the differentiable inner loop with a functional inner optimizer, the implicit-diff wrapper at torchopt.diff.implicit_diff (or torchopt.implicit_diff) applied over the inner fixed point, and the inner optimizer constructed via torchopt.MetaSGD for the secondary MetaSGD arm or plain torchopt.SGD otherwise. Hessian-vector products double through torch.autograd.grad or functorch jvp/vjp.\\n\\nFallback path (Tier B): the same algorithmic identities implemented via torch.func.functional_call plus torch.func.grad plus torch.func.vmap and an in-script CG loop. The two paths are connected by a single meta_solver.py module with a uniform interface so swapping Tier A for Tier B is a one-file change. A solver-equivalence test on the toy task asserts gradient relative L2 at most 1e-6 between tiers to catch TorchOpt API drift.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\nSynthetic only (network disabled, no online mirror). Three tasks.\\n\\n- Toy linear regression with d=10, sigma=0.1, K_inner=1, K_shot=10, K_query=20. Unique in that the meta-gradient has a closed form; the gradient-fidelity threshold of relative L2 at most 1e-2 is defined here against the analytic oracle.\\n- Sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 protocol), amplitude in [0.1, 5.0], phase in [0, pi], K_shot=10, K_query=10, 4 inner updates. MLP 2x40 ReLU.\\n- Synthetic Gaussian-mixture 5-way 5-shot classification with controllable inter-class margin, K_query=15 per class, K_inner=5, 4-layer ConvNet (MAML MiniImageNet style), 28x28 grayscale inputs.\\n\\n## Networks and optimizers\\n\\nMLP 2x40 ReLU (about 1700 params) for regression; 4-layer ConvNet with BN and max-pool (about 110000 params) for classification. Inner SGD with per-parameter inner LR (torchopt.MetaSGD primary; torch.optim.SGD fallback). Outer Adam at outer LR 1e-3. Inner LR alpha in {0.01, 0.04, 0.1}. K_inner in {1, 5, 10}. Damping lambda in {0, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1}, default 1e-2. Neumann depth K in {3, 5, 10}, default 5. CG iters T in {5, 10, 20}, default 10, tolerance 1e-5.\\n\\n## Controls\\n\\nThree seeds {0, 1, 2}. Separate PRNG streams for data generation, task sampling, and inner initialization. Deterministic inner init given seed. Sanity probe with alpha=0 must reproduce FOMAML. Method parity table across all arms. NaN/divergence detector. Tier A versus Tier B solver-equivalence test. REPTILE control.\\n\\n## Metrics\\n\\nGradient fidelity (relative L2 against analytic oracle on toy, against unrolled-K second-order on sine). Inner-loss trajectory: L_query versus inner adaptation step on the 600-task held-out split. Meta-test performance: MSE (sine), 5-way accuracy in pp (classification), both with 95 percent CIs from 3 seeds via student t. Per-iteration wall-clock split into inner and outer segments via time.perf_counter. Peak resident memory via resource.getrusage plus an instrumented accounting of retain_graph bytes. Stability: fraction of divergence events plus per-event log.\\n\\n## Analysis plan\\n\\nEight deliverables: gradient-fidelity table; meta-test learning curves; wall-clock-corrected curves; Pareto front over (peak meta-test, wall-clock per iter, peak memory); lambda / K / T ablations; stability diagnostics; memory-scaling plot versus K_inner; solver equivalence check. Decision rule for success: implicit arm wins iff its 95 percent CI on meta-test overlaps EXPLICIT-MAML AND its wall-clock per iter OR peak memory is strictly better than EXPLICIT-MAML. Multi-axis Pareto wins highlighted.\\n\\n## Success thresholds\\n\\n- Toy gradient fidelity at most 1e-2 relative L2 between the implicit meta-gradient and the analytic K_inner=1 meta-gradient (IMPLICIT-EXACT), with monotone decrease in K (Neumann) and T (CG) for the truncated arms.\\n- Sine-wave and synthetic-classification: 95 percent CI overlap with EXPLICIT-MAML AND strict improvement on wall-clock per iter OR peak memory. Pareto-front dominance is mandatory in the analysis.\\n\\n## Feasibility\\n\\nCPU-only, 24 cores, no GPU, overnight (at most 12 hours total). Per-task iteration caps: 5000 (toy), 10000 (sine), 20000 (classification), per arm per seed. Asymptotic per-iteration estimates: about 10 ms toy, about 100 ms sine, about 500 ms classification on a 24-core box, fitting within at most 50 s / 17 min / 2.8 hours per arm per seed. Full sweep worst-case at most about 12 hours wall-clock. Verification setup step: a single (IMPLICIT-CG, sine, seed=0) run of 1000 iterations should complete in at most 2 minutes; otherwise drop meta-batch to 2 before launching.\\n\\nREPTILE mirrors the same caps. The IMPLICIT-EXACT arm runs only on the toy. The factorized lambda x K / lambda x T ablation is run at a single alpha and single K_inner on the sine and classification arms to stay within feasibility. Out-of-default alpha and K_inner are explored on the toy only.\\n\\n## What we do not claim\\n\\nNo online data. No MiniImageNet or CIFAR-FS or Omniglot numbers. No fabricated empirical results. No claim about TorchOpt symbols beyond `assume v0.9-style:` tags for everything not locally verified (TorchOpt is not installed in the present bubblewrap and cannot be installed). The synthetic Gaussian mixture is explicitly *not* a benchmark stand-in for MiniImageNet; it is an algorithmic-differential probe.\\n\\n## File layout for portable execution\\n\\n/workspace/top-level with `meta_solver.py` (single Tier A/B interface), `tasks/`, `nets/`, `baselines/`, `arms/`, `analysis/`, `run_sweep.py`, and `tests/`. Details in section 11 of final_design.md.\\n\\n## Reproduction recipe\\n\\n1. Install Python 3.10+ with PyTorch 2.0+ and optionally TorchOpt.\\n2. Run the solver-equivalence test.\\n3. Run the sanity sweep on toy.\\n4. Run the sine sweep (at most 2 hours).\\n5. Run the classification sweep (at most 9 hours).\\n6. Run all analysis scripts and apply the decision rule.\\n\\nIf the sanity sweep produces gradient-relative-L2 greater than 1e-2 or the alpha=0 sanity probe fails to return FOMAML, STOP and debug before launching the full sweep.\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nFinal design complete. Awaiting researcher acknowledgement; no execution performed.\\nSUMMARY\\necho \\\"wrote $(wc -l < /workspace/notes/design_summary.md) lines to /workspace/notes/design_summary.md\\\"\\necho \\\"---\\\"\\nls -la /workspace/notes/\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-55fe6f7a-6b70-4c1a-bbea-f5f46bbf43c7", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 54}
